{"id":7,"role":"Tool","content":"C:\\github\\YangMills\\README.md\n-- 748 lines, 49206 bytes --\n  1 | # Yang-Mills Millennium Prize — Lean 4 Formalization\n  2 | \n  3 | A Lean 4 formalization effort around the mathematical foundations of quantum\n  4 | Yang-Mills theory, undertaken with the long-term goal of a formally verified\n  5 | proof of the Yang-Mills existence and mass gap conjecture (Clay Millennium\n  6 | Prize Problem).\n  7 | \n  8 | **Status as of this update: the Millennium Prize theorem is NOT proved.**\n  9 | The top-level statement currently depends on an axiom\n 10 | (`mass_gap_axiom`) that is logically equivalent to the conjecture itself.\n 11 | Any derivation from that axiom is circular and should not be read as\n 12 | progress on the open problem. See \"Status of the Millennium Prize Theorem\"\n 13 | below before reading anything else in this document.\n 14 | \n 15 | > **Maintenance note:** this README must be kept in sync with the actual\n 16 | > Lean source on every session that touches the proof state (new lemmas,\n 17 | > new axioms, new sorries, sorries closed, axioms removed). Do not let\n 18 | > claims here drift ahead of — or behind — what `lake build` and\n 19 | > `#print axioms` actually report. See \"Keeping this README honest\" at the\n 20 | > bottom.\n 21 | \n 22 | ---\n 23 | \n 24 | ## Status of the Millennium Prize Theorem\n 25 | \n 26 | **Not proved.** The formalization currently rests on **six** axioms (not\n 27 | four — `MassGapProof.lean`'s module docstring says \"four axioms\" and then\n 28 | lists five; that docstring needs fixing too), one of which (`mass_gap_axiom`)\n 29 | directly encodes the conjecture being proved. Any theorem chain that\n 30 | terminates in this axiom is not a proof of anything new — it is a\n 31 | restatement.\n 32 | \n 33 | | Axiom | Declared in | What it stands in for | Status / concern |\n 34 | |---|---|---|---|\n 35 | | `peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette` | `PeterWeyl.lean` | Peter–Weyl theorem + Clebsch–Gordan decomposition for the plaquette Boltzmann factor **and** for products of characters of the same group element (across-plaquette CG) **and** dual (contragredient) representations | Neither is in Mathlib. Defensible as a cited external theorem *if* correctly applied — needs audit. The axiom was **strengthened** (2026-07-03 session) to also provide the CG decomposition `χ_s(g)·χ_t(g) = ∑_w cg s t w · χ_w(g)` with `cg s t w ≥ 0` (Littlewood–Richardson), which is the key ingredient for combining character expansions across plaquettes that share a link variable. The axiom was **further strengthened** (2026-07-30 session) to also provide a dual map `dual : ι → ι` with `χ_{dual(i)}(g) = conj(χ_i(g))` (the contragredient representation has conjugate character), which is needed to handle inverted links in the plaquette product (`χ(g⁻¹) = conj(χ(g)) = χ_{dual}(g)` by `repCharacter_inv`). Two new lemmas proved from the strengthened axiom (0 sorries, 0 custom axioms): `charProduct_PD` (product of two chars is PD via CG) and `charProduct_finset_decomp` (finite product of chars of the same element decomposes as a non-negative-weighted sum of single characters via iterated CG). |\n 36 | | `transferMatrixPositivity_axiom` | `ReflectionPositivity.lean` (**confirmed** — not a `sorry`, and not in `TransferMatrix.lean` despite `Overview.lean` implying otherwise) | Positivity of `∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀` for the periodic-lattice transfer matrix | Docstring gives a real justification chain (plaquette PD ⇒ transfer matrix positive ⇒ integral nonnegative). All abstract sub-steps are now proved (see \"Suggested next step\" below). The clean factorization `osG_thetaG_factorization` (0 sorries, 0 axioms) shows the axiom is equivalent to `∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β S_W(U)) dμ ≥ 0`. The full Boltzmann factor `exp(-β S_W)` is proved PD on the full link group by `boltzmannFactorPD` (modulo Peter–Weyl). **Key obstruction**: this integral is NOT the standard PD quadratic form `∫∫ f(g)·conj(f(h))·K(g⁻¹h) dμ dμ ≥ 0` (which follows from PD-ness of `K` and is proved as `integralOperator_nonneg`). It is a *single* integral `∫ f(g)·f(θg)·K(g) dμ` with the geometric reflection `θ` and `K` evaluated at `g` (not `g⁻¹h`). PD-ness of `K` does not imply this is non-negative; the Peter–Weyl character expansion of `K` and character orthogonality are needed to decompose the integrand into `|Fourier coefficients|²`. This is a fundamental mathematical gap, not just formalization work. |\n 37 | | `os_reconstruction_theorem` | `OSAxioms.lean` | Osterwalder–Schrader reconstruction (OS axioms ⇒ Wightman QFT) | Established published math, not in Mathlib. Only sound to invoke on objects that actually satisfy the OS axioms — depends on the continuum limit existing (see next row). |\n 38 | | `continuum_limit_exists` | `ContinuumLimit.lean` | Existence of the lattice a→0 continuum limit (Balaban RG / stochastic quantization) | **This axiom *is* the open mathematical core of the problem.** Not a placeholder for something routine — it's the thing nobody has proved. |\n 39 | | `mass_gap_axiom` | `MassGap.lean` | Positivity of the continuum mass gap | **This is the conjecture itself.** `yang_mills_existence_and_mass_gap` in `MassGapProof.lean` pulls the gap and its positivity directly from this axiom (`let mg := mass_gap_axiom a ha`) without deriving anything from the lattice work — the clearest concrete illustration of the circularity in the codebase. Must not be used in any theorem claimed as a \"proof\" of the Millennium Prize result. |\n 40 | | `characterOrthogonality` | `PositiveDefinite.lean` | Schur orthogonality for irreducible unitary representations of a compact group | Not in Mathlib. The **Great Orthogonality Theorem**: `∫ χ_λ(g)·conj(χ_μ(g)) dμ = δ_{λμ}` for normalized Haar measure. This is the key ingredient needed to turn the Peter–Weyl character expansion of the Boltzmann factor into the `|Fourier coefficient|²` decomposition of the reflection-positivity integral. Defensible as a cited external theorem. |\n 41 | \n 42 | Any file, comment, or summary claiming the top-level theorem is \"proved\"\n 43 | while it depends on `mass_gap_axiom` is wrong and should be corrected.\n 44 | \n 45 | ### Suggested next step: wire the abstract lemmas into the lattice setup to close `transferMatrixPositivity_axiom`\n 46 | \n 47 | Unlike the other four axioms, this one looks achievable with what's already\n 48 | built. Its docstring lays out the chain: the plaquette Boltzmann factor is\n 49 | positive-definite (`plaquetteBoltzmannPD`, proved modulo the Peter–Weyl\n 50 | axiom) ⟹ the transfer matrix built from it is a positive operator ⟹ the\n 51 | integral is nonnegative. All abstract sub-steps are now proved:\n 52 | \n 53 | 1. `PositiveDefinite.integral` — an integral average of PD functions is PD\n 54 |    (closes \"integrate out interior links ⟹ PD kernel\").\n 55 | 2. `PositiveDefinite.integralOperator_nonneg` — a PD kernel on a compact\n 56 |    group defines a positive integral operator\n 57 |    (`∫∫ f(x)·conj(f(y))·K(x⁻¹y) dμ dμ ≥ 0`; closes \"PD kernel ⟹ positive\n 58 |    operator\").  The proof approximates the integral by Riemann sums and\n 59 |    controls the error via uniform continuity on `G × G`.\n 60 | 3. `PositiveDefiniteKernel.integralOperator_nonneg` — the **Mercer-type**\n 61 |    generalization: a continuous Mercer-PD kernel on a compact space (no group\n 62 |    structure needed) defines a positive integral operator.  This removes the\n 63 |    group-structure requirement that obstructs the group-theoretic version.\n 64 | 4. `boltzmannFactorPD` (`BoltzmannFactor.lean`) — the **full Boltzmann factor**\n 65 |    `exp(-β S_W) = ∏ exp(-S_p)` is PD on the full link-variable group, via\n 66 |    `plaquetteContributionPD` (each plaquette factor PD by `comp_hom` from\n 67 |    `plaquetteBoltzmannPD_inv`) and `PositiveDefinite.finprod` (n-ary Schur\n 68 |    product).  0 sorries, 0 axioms beyond Peter–Weyl.\n 69 | 5. `osG_thetaG_factorization` (`ReflectionPositivity.lean`) — the clean\n 70 |    algebraic identity `osG(U)·osG(θU) = f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β S_W(U))`, showing\n 71 |    the axiom is equivalent to `∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β S_W) dμ ≥ 0`.  0 sorries,\n 72 |    0 axioms.\n 73 | \n 74 | All are 0 sorries, 0 custom axioms (only Peter–Weyl for the plaquette PD).\n 75 | The **remaining** work is the fundamental mathematical gap: the integral\n 76 | `∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β S_W) dμ` is NOT the standard PD quadratic form\n 77 | `∫∫ f(g)·conj(f(h))·K(g⁻¹h) dμ dμ ≥ 0` — it is a single integral with the\n 78 | geometric reflection `θ` and `K` evaluated at `g` (not `g⁻¹h`).  PD-ness of\n 79 | `K` does not imply this is non-negative; the Peter–Weyl character expansion\n 80 | of `K` and character orthogonality are needed to decompose the integrand\n 81 | into `|Fourier coefficients|²`.  Closing it, even leaving\n 82 | `peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette` as an axiom, would be a genuine reduction\n 83 | in the codebase's assumption count (six axioms → five).\n 84 | \n 85 | ### The character-orthogonality path to closing `transferMatrixPositivity_axiom`\n 86 | \n 87 | The newly added `characterOrthogonality` axiom (in `PositiveDefinite.lean`)\n 88 | provides the key missing ingredient for the reflection-positivity argument.\n 89 | The path is:\n 90 | \n 91 | 1. The full Boltzmann factor `K = exp(-β S_W)` has a Peter–Weyl character\n 92 |    expansion `K = ∑_λ a_λ χ_λ` with `a_λ ≥ 0` (axiomatized by\n 93 |    `peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette`, applied plaquette-by-plaquette and\n 94 |    multiplied via `boltzmannFactorPD`).\n 95 | 2. The reflection-positivity integral `∫ f(U)·f(θU)·K(U) dμ` (shown\n 96 |    equivalent to the axiom by `osG_thetaG_factorization`) becomes, after\n 97 |    substituting the character expansion, a sum of terms\n 98 |    `∑_λ a_λ · ∫ f(U)·f(θU)·χ_λ(U) dμ`.\n 99 | 3. Using the reflection symmetry of the Haar measure (`μ(θA) = μ(A)`) and\n100 |    character orthogonality (`characterOrthogonality`), each term\n101 |    `∫ f(U)·f(θU)·χ_λ(U) dμ` can be rewritten as `|∫ f(U)·χ_λ(U) dμ|² ≥ 0`\n102 |    (a squared Fourier coefficient).\n103 | 4. Since `a_λ ≥ 0` and `|·|² ≥ 0`, the entire sum is non-negative.\n104 | \n105 | Steps 1–2 are already formalized (`boltzmannFactorPD`,\n106 | `osG_thetaG_factorization`).  Step 3 requires the `characterOrthogonality`\n107 | axiom (now added) plus the reflection-invariance of the Haar measure\n108 | (`μ(θA) = μ(A)`, which follows from the Haar measure being invariant under\n109 | all measure-preserving maps, including the reflection `θ`).  Step 4 is\n110 | trivial algebra.  The remaining formalization work is the concrete wiring\n111 | of steps 3–4 into the lattice-gauge-theory setup.\n112 | \n113 | **Precise analysis (2026-06-29 session):** the naive path above (steps 1–4\n114 | at the level of the full Boltzmann factor) does NOT work directly, because\n115 | `χ_λ(θU) ≠ conj(χ_λ(U))` in general — the reflection `θ` is not group\n116 | inversion.  The correct approach works at the level of the **transfer matrix\n117 | kernel** `K_TM(u, U⁻)`, which must decompose as\n118 | `∑_λ a_λ Φ_λ(u) · conj(Φ_λ(θ⁻⁰(U⁻, u⁰)))` with `a_λ ≥ 0`.  The change of\n119 | variables `U⁻ ↦ θ⁻⁰(U⁻, u⁰)` (measure-preserving by\n120 | `reflectLinkVariable_measurePreserving`) then turns the integral into a sum\n121 | of `|Fourier coefficients|² ≥ 0`.  The key obstruction to formalizing this\n122 | kernel decomposition is that the interface Boltzmann factor is a **product\n123 | of multiple plaquette factors**, and combining their character expansions\n124 | requires the **Clebsch–Gordan decomposition** for products of characters of\n125 | the same link variable — not currently axiomatized.  See\n126 | `docs/gap_analysis.md` for the full analysis.\n127 | \n128 | **Further analysis (2026-07-02 session):** a detailed investigation of whether\n129 | the abstract lemma `character_expansion_positivity` (proved 2026-07-01, 0\n130 | sorries, 0 axioms) can be directly wired into the lattice setup revealed\n131 | **three interconnected obstructions** that prevent direct application:\n132 | \n133 | 1. **`θ⁻⁰` depends on both `x` and `y`.**  The lemma requires `θ : Y → X` (a\n134 |    function of `y` only), but `θ⁻⁰(U⁻, u⁰)` depends on `u⁰`, which is part of\n135 |    `x = u = (u⁺, u⁰)`.\n136 | 2. **The pushforward of `μ⁻` by `θ⁻⁰(·, u⁰)` is singular.**  Even generalized\n137 |    to allow `x`-dependent `θ`, the pushforward is `μ⁺ × δ_{σ(u⁰)}` (a point\n138 |    mass at the reflected interface config), NOT the full `μ⁺⁰ = μ⁺ × μ⁰`.\n139 |    The change of variables gives an integral over a slice, not the full space.\n140 | 3. **The `σ` reflection on interface time-like links.**  The reflection\n141 |    inverts interface time-like links (`g ↦ g⁻¹`), causing `χ(g)²` instead of\n142 |    `|χ(g)|²` in the separable form.  The result would be\n143 |    `∑ a_i ∫ A_i(u⁰) conj(A_i(σ(u⁰))) dμ⁰(u⁰)`, which is NOT necessarily\n144 |    non-negative.\n145 | \n146 | **Conclusion:** `character_expansion_positivity` is NOT the right scaffold for\n147 | the lattice case.  The correct approach (from the Osterwalder–Seiler proof) is\n148 | the **operator-theoretic** argument: show the transfer matrix `T = B* · B` for\n149 | some operator `B` defined via the character expansion (Peter–Weyl + CG), then\n150 | `⟨g, Tg⟩ = ‖Bg‖² ≥ 0`.  This requires (a) a Clebsch–Gordan axiom for products\n151 | of characters of the same group element, and (b) the full combinatorial wiring\n152 | of the interface plaquette expansion.  See `docs/gap_analysis.md` §\"Precise\n153 | analysis of why `character_expansion_positivity` does NOT directly apply\" for\n154 | the full analysis.\n155 | \n156 | **Progress (2026-07-03 session):** Step (a) is now complete.  The\n157 | `peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette` axiom has been **strengthened** to also\n158 | provide the Clebsch–Gordan decomposition for character products:\n159 | `χ_s(g)·χ_t(g) = ∑_w cg s t w · χ_w(g)` with `cg s t w ≥ 0` (Littlewood–\n160 | Richardson).  This is the \"across-plaquette\" CG that was identified as the key\n161 | missing ingredient — it allows combining character expansions when the same\n162 | link variable appears in multiple plaquettes.  Two new lemmas proved from the\n163 | strengthened axiom (0 sorries, 0 custom axioms — verified by `#print axioms`):\n164 | `charProduct_PD` (product of two chars is PD via CG) and\n165 | `charProduct_finset_decomp` (finite product of chars of the same element\n166 | decomposes as a non-negative-weighted sum of single characters via iterated\n167 | CG).  Two further lemmas proved from the strengthened axiom (0 sorries, 0\n168 | custom axioms — verified by `#print axioms`): `charSum_product_decomp`\n169 | (product of two non-negative-weighted char sums decomposes as a\n170 | non-negative-weighted char sum via CG) and `charSum_finprod_decomp` (finite\n171 | product of non-negative-weighted char sums decomposes as a\n172 | non-negative-weighted char sum via iterated CG).  The axiom count remains\n173 | **six** (the strengthening enriches an existing axiom, it does not add a new\n174 | one).  Step (b) — formalizing the operator `B` and showing `T = B* · B` —\n175 | remains the major formalization effort.\n176 | \n177 | ## What is actually proved (no sorry, no axiom)\n178 | \n179 | ### SU(N) and general algebra\n180 | - `CompactSpace (SU N)`, `SecondCountableTopology (SU N)`\n181 | - `trace_commutator_zero`, `trace_mul_rotate`, `trace_unitary_conj_invariant`,\n182 |   `trace_sq_invariant`, `jacobi_identity`\n183 | - `adjointAction_preserves_trace_sq`, `adjointAction_preserves_skewHermitian`,\n184 |   `adjointAction_preserves_traceless`\n185 | - `lagrangian_gauge_invariant`\n186 | \n187 | ### Positive-definite function algebra\n188 | - `PositiveDefinite.add`, `.smul_nonneg`, `.conj_inv`, `.zero`, `.one`\n189 | - `PositiveDefinite.mul` (Schur product theorem / Hadamard product of PSD\n190 |   matrices)\n191 | - `PositiveDefinite.pow`, `.tendsto`, `.prod`, `.sum`, `.sum'`\n192 | - `repCharacter_positiveDefinite`, `fundamentalCharacter_positiveDefinite`,\n193 |   `reTrace_positiveDefinite`\n194 | - `IsUnitaryRepresentation`, `repCharacter`, `repCharacter_inv`\n195 |   (`χ(g⁻¹) = conj(χ(g))`)\n196 | - `IsIrreducible` — a unitary representation is irreducible if the only\n197 |   invariant subspaces are `{0}` and the whole space\n198 | - `characterOrthogonality` (axiom) — Schur orthogonality for irreducible\n199 |   unitary representations of a compact group: `∫ χ_λ·conj(χ_μ) dμ = δ_{λμ}`\n200 |   for normalized Haar measure.  Not in Mathlib; the key ingredient for the\n201 |   `|Fourier coefficient|²` decomposition of the reflection-positivity integral.\n202 | - `exp_reTrace_positiveDefinite` (single-link Boltzmann factor; proved\n203 |   unconditionally via the power-series / `PositiveDefinite.tendsto` argument —\n204 |   no axiom)\n205 | - `plaquetteBoltzmannPD` (depends on the `peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette`\n206 |   axiom above — flagged, not unconditional)\n207 | - `plaquetteBoltzmannPD_inv` (`PeterWeyl.lean`) — the plaquette Boltzmann\n208 |   factor with inverse links `exp(c·Re Tr(g₁ g₂ g₃⁻¹ g₄⁻¹))` is PD on\n209 |   `SU(N)⁴` (the version needed for the actual lattice plaquette product;\n210 |   depends on the Peter–Weyl axiom)\n211 | - `charProduct_PD` (`PeterWeyl.lean`) — the product of two characters of the\n212 |   *same* group element `χ_s(g)·χ_t(g)` is PD, via the Clebsch–Gordan\n213 |   decomposition `χ_s·χ_t = ∑_w cg s t w · χ_w` with `cg s t w ≥ 0` (provided\n214 |   by the strengthened `peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette` axiom).  0 sorries,\n215 |   0 custom axioms beyond Peter–Weyl.  Verified by `#print axioms` (only\n216 |   `propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`).\n217 | - `charProduct_finset_decomp` (`PeterWeyl.lean`) — a finite product of\n218 |   characters of the same group element `∏_{i ∈ s} χ_i(g)` decomposes as a\n219 |   non-negative-weighted sum of single characters `∑_w coeff w · χ_w(g)` with\n220 |   `coeff w ≥ 0`, via iterated Clebsch–Gordan.  This is the key algebraic\n221 |   ingredient for the transfer-matrix kernel decomposition: when a single link\n222 |   variable appears in multiple interface plaquettes, the product of the\n223 |   character expansions produces a product of characters of that link, which\n224 |   this lemma reduces to a single non-negative sum.  0 sorries, 0 custom axioms\n225 |   beyond Peter–Weyl.  Verified by `#print axioms` (only `propext`,\n226 |   `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`).\n227 | - `charSum_product_decomp` (`PeterWeyl.lean`) — the product of two\n228 |   non-negative-weighted sums of characters `(∑_a c1 a · χ_a) · (∑_b c2 b · χ_b)`\n229 |   decomposes as a non-negative-weighted sum of single characters\n230 |   `∑_w coeff w · χ_w` with `coeff w = ∑_{a,b} c1 a · c2 b · cg a b w ≥ 0`,\n231 |   via Clebsch–Gordan.  This is the key algebraic ingredient for combining\n232 |   character expansions across plaquettes that share a link variable.  0 sorries,\n233 |   0 custom axioms beyond Peter–Weyl.  Verified by `#print axioms` (only\n234 |   `propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`).\n235 | - `charSum_finprod_decomp` (`PeterWeyl.lean`) — a finite product of\n236 |   non-negative-weighted sums of characters\n237 |   `∏_{a ∈ s} (∑_w f a w · χ_w(g))` decomposes as a non-negative-weighted sum\n238 |   of single characters `∑_w coeff w · χ_w(g)` with `coeff w ≥ 0`, via iterated\n239 |   Clebsch–Gordan (induction on `s` using `charSum_product_decomp`).  This is\n240 |   the key lemma for the interface Boltzmann factor decomposition: after\n241 |   collecting characters by link variable, each link's contribution is a\n242 |   non-negative-weighted sum of characters, and this lemma shows the product\n243 |   over all links is again a non-negative-weighted sum of characters.  0\n244 |   sorries, 0 custom axioms beyond Peter–Weyl.  Verified by `#print axioms`\n245 |   (only `pr