{"id":10,"role":"Tool","content":"path:C:\\github\\YangMills\\docs\\gap_analysis.md\n-- 797 lines, 46178 bytes --\n  1 | # Gap Analysis: Correct Reflection Positivity Proof\n  2 | \n  3 | ## Current Status\n  4 | \n  5 | The reflection positivity proof for the Wilson action on a finite periodic lattice\n  6 | (`gibbsExpectationPeriodic_reflection_positive` in `ReflectionPositivity.lean`)\n  7 | is complete except for the final positivity step (the integral of G·θG).\n  8 | \n  9 | ### ✅ Correction applied (2026-06-28)\n 10 | \n 11 | The `TransferMatrix.lean` file has been rewritten with the **correct** transfer matrix\n 12 | definitions.  The previous approach (using `G_plus` with negative links set to 1) was\n 13 | mathematically incorrect.  The file now contains:\n 14 | \n 15 | - **`reflectToPosInterface`**: The map θ⁻⁰ from negative+interface to positive+interface\n 16 |   configurations via the full geometric reflection.\n 17 | - **`g_posInterface`**: The function `g(u) = f(u)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(u)/2)` for `u` in the\n 18 |   positive+interface space.\n 19 | - **`transferMatrixCorrect`**: The correct transfer matrix T acting on PosInterfaceConfig:\n 20 |   `(T ψ)(u) = ∫ ψ(θ⁻⁰(U⁻,u⁰))·exp(-β·(S_OS⁻(U⁻)+S_OS_int(u,U⁻))/2) dμ⁻(U⁻)`\n 21 | - **`integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg`**: The key identity (proof is `sorry`).\n 22 | - **`transferMatrixCorrect_positive`**: Positivity of T (proof is `sorry`, requires Peter-Weyl).\n 23 | - **`integral_G_thetaG_nonneg'`**: The final positivity result under the hypothesis.\n 24 | \n 25 | The old incorrect `transferMatrix_identity` has been removed.  See `docs/found_issues.md`\n 26 | for details on the mathematical error.\n 27 | \n 28 | ### What is proved (no gaps):\n 29 | ### What is proved (no gaps):\n 30 | \n 31 | 1. **Action decomposition**: $S_W = S^+_{OS} + S^-_{OS} + S^0_{OS}$ (OS plaquette decomposition)\n 32 |    - `total_decomposition_os_periodic`\n 33 |    - `neg_action_reflection_os_periodic`: $S^-_{OS}[U] = S^+_{OS}[\\theta U]$\n 34 |    - `interface_action_reflection_symmetric_os_periodic`: $S^0_{OS}[\\theta U] = S^0_{OS}[U]$\n 35 | \n 36 | 2. **Algebraic factorization**: $\\exp(-\\beta S_W) = G(U) \\cdot G(\\theta U)$ where\n 37 |    $G(U) = f(U) \\exp(-\\beta S^+_{OS}(U)) \\exp(-\\beta S^0_{OS}(U)/2)$\n 38 |    - `h_factorization`, `h_integrand_eq`\n 39 | \n 40 | 3. **Reduction to integral**: $\\langle f \\cdot \\theta f \\rangle = \\int G(U) G(\\theta U) \\, d\\mu_0(U)$\n 41 | \n 42 | ### The remaining gap:\n 43 | \n 44 | $$\\int_{\\text{config}} G(U) \\cdot G(\\theta U) \\, d\\mu_0(U) \\ge 0$$\n 45 | \n 46 | where $\\mu_0$ is the product Haar measure on $\\text{SU}(N)^{\\text{links}}$.\n 47 | \n 48 | ### ✅ Key identity PROVEN (2026-06-28)\n 49 | \n 50 | The measure-theoretic identity\n 51 | $$\\int G(U) \\cdot G(\\theta U) \\, d\\mu_0(U) = \\int_{\\text{PosInt}} g(u) \\cdot (Tg)(u) \\, d\\mu^{\\pm 0}(u)$$\n 52 | is fully proven in `TransferMatrix.lean` (`integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg`, 0 sorries).\n 53 | This reduces the problem to showing $\\langle g, Tg \\rangle \\ge 0$.\n 54 | \n 55 | ### ❌ Transfer matrix positivity — abstract sub-steps proved, wiring remaining\n 56 | \n 57 | The transfer matrix $T$ is positive **if and only if** its integral kernel is a\n 58 | positive-definite function on the group of link variables. The kernel is a product of\n 59 | plaquette Boltzmann factors $\\exp(c \\cdot \\operatorname{Re} \\operatorname{Tr}(U_{\\partial p}))$.\n 60 | \n 61 | **Both abstract sub-steps of the positivity chain are now proved** (in\n 62 | `PositiveDefiniteIntegral.lean`, 0 sorries, 0 custom axioms):\n 63 | \n 64 | 1. `PositiveDefinite.integral` — an integral average of PD functions is PD\n 65 |    (closes \"integrate out interior links ⟹ the resulting kernel is PD\").\n 66 | 2. `PositiveDefinite.integralOperator_nonneg` — a PD kernel $K$ on a compact\n 67 |    group $G$ defines a positive integral operator:\n 68 |    $\\iint f(x)\\,\\overline{f(y)}\\,K(x^{-1}y)\\,d\\mu(x)\\,d\\mu(y) \\ge 0$.\n 69 |    The proof approximates the integral by Riemann sums (each non-negative by\n 70 |    `PositiveDefinite.sum_nonneg_of_map`) and controls the error via uniform\n 71 |    continuity on $G \\times G$.\n 72 | \n 73 | **Approach (a) — Mercer-type PD kernel theory — is now also built** (same file,\n 74 | 0 sorries, 0 custom axioms).  This removes the group-structure requirement:\n 75 | \n 76 | 3. `PositiveDefiniteKernel` — a kernel $K : X \\to X \\to \\mathbb{C}$ is\n 77 |    positive-definite in the Mercer sense: $\\sum c_i \\overline{c_j} K(x_i, x_j)\n 78 |    \\ge 0$ for every finite set and coefficients.  No group structure on $X$.\n 79 | 4. `PositiveDefiniteKernel.sum_nonneg_of_map` — the quadratic form is\n 80 |    non-negative even under a non-injective index map.\n 81 | 5. `PositiveDefiniteKernel.integralOperator_nonneg` — a *continuous* Mercer-PD\n 82 |    kernel on a compact space defines a positive integral operator, with no\n 83 |    Haar-measure invariance or group structure needed.  This is the strictly more\n 84 |    general version of (2); `PositiveDefinite.toPositiveDefiniteKernel` shows the\n 85 |    group-theoretic notion embeds into the Mercer one.\n 86 | \n 87 | **Building blocks for the full Boltzmann factor** are now proved in\n 88 | `PositiveDefinite.lean` (0 sorries):\n 89 | \n 90 | 6. `PositiveDefinite.comp_mulEquiv` — PD is preserved by group isomorphisms\n 91 |    (permute/rearrange factors of a product group).\n 92 | 7. `PositiveDefinite.fst` / `.snd` — a PD function on one factor, viewed as a\n 93 |    function on a product that ignores the other factor, is PD (extension by\n 94 |    constants).\n 95 | 8. `PositiveDefinite.finprod` — a finite product of PD functions on the same\n 96 |    group is PD (n-ary Schur product theorem).\n 97 | \n 98 | These are the abstract ingredients for promoting the single-plaquette result\n 99 | `plaquetteBoltzmannPD` to PD-ness of the full Boltzmann factor\n100 | $\\exp(-\\beta S_W) = \\prod_p \\exp(\\beta\\,\\operatorname{Re}\\operatorname{Tr}(U_{\\partial p}))$\n101 | on the entire link-variable group.  The remaining combinatorial wiring (which\n102 | links belong to which plaquette, permuting factors into position) is\n103 | lattice-specific and not yet formalized.\n104 | \n105 | **Additional infrastructure** proved in `PositiveDefinite.lean` and\n106 | `PeterWeyl.lean` (0 sorries, 0 custom axioms beyond the Peter–Weyl axiom):\n107 | \n108 | 9. `PositiveDefinite.comp_hom` — PD is preserved by group *homomorphisms*\n109 |    (generalizes `comp_mulEquiv` from isomorphisms to arbitrary homomorphisms,\n110 |    e.g. coordinate projections from a product group to a sub-product).\n111 | 10. `repCharacter_inv` — for a unitary representation, the character satisfies\n112 |     $\\chi(g^{-1}) = \\overline{\\chi(g)}$ (from $\\rho(g^{-1}) = \\rho(g)^{-1} =\n113 |     \\rho(g)^H$ and $\\operatorname{Tr}(M^H) = \\overline{\\operatorname{Tr}(M)}$).\n114 | 11. `plaquetteBoltzmannPD_inv` — the plaquette Boltzmann factor with **inverse\n115 |     links** $\\exp(c \\cdot \\operatorname{Re}\\operatorname{Tr}(g_1 g_2 g_3^{-1}\n116 |     g_4^{-1}))$ is PD on $\\text{SU}(N)^4$.  This is the version needed for the\n117 |     actual lattice plaquette product $U(n,\\mu) \\cdot U(n{+}e_\\mu,\\nu) \\cdot\n118 |     U(n{+}e_\\mu{+}e_\\nu,\\mu)^{-1} \\cdot U(n{+}e_\\nu,\\nu)^{-1}$, which has\n119 |     inverses on the 3rd and 4th links (orientation reversal).  The proof\n120 |     substitutes $(g_1, g_2, g_3^{-1}, g_4^{-1})$ into the Peter–Weyl axiom,\n121 |     then replaces $\\chi_u(g_3^{-1}) = \\overline{\\chi_u(g_3)}$ and\n122 |     $\\chi_v(g_4^{-1}) = \\overline{\\chi_v(g_4)}$ via `repCharacter_inv`; each\n123 |     factor $\\chi_s \\cdot \\chi_t \\cdot \\overline{\\chi_u} \\cdot \\overline{\\chi_v}$\n124 |     is PD by `charProduct4_inv_positiveDefinite` (using `PositiveDefinite.conj`\n125 |     on the 3rd and 4th factors), and the sum with non-negative coefficients is\n126 |     PD by `PositiveDefinite.sum`.\n127 | \n128 | **Promotion to the full link-variable group** is now proved in\n129 | `BoltzmannFactor.lean` (0 sorries, 0 custom axioms beyond the Peter–Weyl axiom;\n130 | full `lake build` clean):\n131 | \n132 | 12. `plaquetteProjection` — the group homomorphism\n133 |     `LinkVariable (SU N) Λ →* ((SU N × SU N) × SU N) × SU N` extracting the four\n134 |     link variables around a plaquette `(n, μ, ν)`.  It is a homomorphism because\n135 |     the group operation on `LinkVariable` is pointwise (the product group\n136 |     `SU(N)^{Λ × Fin 4}`) and the operation on `SU(N)^4` is componentwise; both\n137 |     `map_one'` and `map_mul'` hold by `rfl` (definitional pointwise structure).\n138 | 13. `plaquetteFactorPD` — the plaquette Boltzmann factor\n139 |     $\\exp((\\beta/N)\\,\\operatorname{Re}\\operatorname{Tr}(U_{\\partial p}))$ is PD on\n140 |     the **full** link-variable group `LinkVariable (SU N) Λ`.  This is\n141 |     `plaquetteBoltzmannPD_inv` (item 11) composed with `plaquetteProjection` via\n142 |     `PositiveDefinite.comp_hom` (item 9); the composition equals the plaquette\n143 |     factor because `plaquetteProduct` is exactly the `g₁ g₂ g₃⁻¹ g₄⁻¹` pattern\n144 |     that `plaquetteBoltzmannPD_inv` expects.\n145 | 14. `plaquetteContributionPD` — the full plaquette contribution\n146 |     $\\exp(-S_p) = \\exp(-\\beta)\\cdot\\exp((\\beta/N)\\,\\operatorname{Re}\\operatorname{Tr}(U_{\\partial p}))$\n147 |     is PD on the full link-variable group, by `PositiveDefinite.smul_nonneg`\n148 |     applied to the non-negative constant $\\exp(-\\beta)$ and `plaquetteFactorPD`.\n149 | 15. `boltzmannFactorPD` — the **full Boltzmann factor**\n150 |     $\\exp(-S_W) = \\prod_{n,\\mu,\\nu} \\exp(-S_p(n,\\mu,\\nu))$ is PD on the full\n151 |     link-variable group `LinkVariable (SU N) Λ`.  The Wilson action is a sum of\n152 |     plaquette contributions, so the Boltzmann factor factorises as a product;\n153 |     each factor is PD by `plaquetteContributionPD` (item 14), and a finite\n154 |     product of PD functions on the same group is PD by\n155 |     `PositiveDefinite.finprod` (item 8, the n-ary Schur product theorem).  The\n156 |     proof flattens the product over `(sites ×ˢ Fin 4) ×ˢ Fin 4` via\n157 |     `Finset.prod_product`, pushes the negation through the nested sums via\n158 |     `Finset.sum_neg_distrib`, and applies `Real.exp_sum` three times.  0\n159 |     sorries, 0 custom axioms beyond the Peter–Weyl axiom, full `lake build`\n160 |     clean (2967 jobs).\n161 | \n162 | **Mercer-PD kernel building blocks** are now proved in\n163 | `PositiveDefiniteIntegral.lean` (0 sorries, 0 custom axioms; full `lake build`\n164 | clean).  These are the algebraic ingredients needed to construct the TM kernel\n165 | as a Mercer-PD kernel from the group-PD `boltzmannFactorPD`:\n166 | \n167 | 16. `PositiveDefiniteKernel.conj_symm` — a Mercer-PD kernel is Hermitian:\n168 |     $K(x, y) = \\overline{K(y, x)}$ (the kernel analogue of\n169 |     `PositiveDefinite.conj_inv`).\n170 | 17. `PositiveDefiniteKernel.one` — the constant-one kernel is Mercer-PD.\n171 | 18. `PositiveDefiniteKernel.matrix_posSemidef` (private) — a Mercer-PD kernel\n172 |     gives a positive-semidefinite matrix on any finite subset (the bridge to\n173 |     `Matrix.PosSemidef.hadamard`).\n174 | 19. `PositiveDefiniteKernel.mul` — the **Schur (Hadamard) product theorem** for\n175 |     Mercer-PD kernels: the pointwise product of two Mercer-PD kernels is\n176 |     Mercer-PD (kernel analogue of `PositiveDefinite.mul`).\n177 | 20. `PositiveDefiniteKernel.smul_nonneg` — non-negative scaling preserves\n178 |     Mercer-PD.\n179 | 21. `PositiveDefiniteKernel.finprod` — a finite product of Mercer-PD kernels is\n180 |     Mercer-PD (n-ary Schur product theorem, kernel analogue of\n181 |     `PositiveDefinite.finprod`).\n182 | 22. `PositiveDefiniteKernel.comp` — Mercer-PD is preserved by composition with a\n183 |     function $f : X \\to Y$ on both arguments: if $K$ is Mercer-PD on $Y$, then\n184 |     $(x, y) \\mapsto K(f(x), f(y))$ is Mercer-PD on $X$.  This is the key\n185 |     operation for composing the Boltzmann-factor kernel with the\n186 |     reflection/projection maps.\n187 | 23. `PositiveDefiniteKernel.continuous_comp` — continuity of a Mercer-PD kernel\n188 |     is preserved by composition with a continuous function on both arguments\n189 |     (needed to apply `integralOperator_nonneg` to the composed TM kernel).\n190 | \n191 | Together with `PositiveDefinite.toPositiveDefiniteKernel` (group-PD → Mercer-PD,\n192 | item 5), these allow the construction chain: group-PD Boltzmann factor →\n193 | Mercer-PD kernel → compose with reflection/projection maps → Mercer-PD TM kernel\n194 | → `integralOperator_nonneg`.  The **remaining** gap is showing that the\n195 | concrete TM kernel (with the geometric reflection map $\\theta^{-0}$) *is*\n196 | Mercer-PD — which still requires the Peter–Weyl character expansion to\n197 | decompose the Boltzmann factor into separable positive terms (approach (c)).\n198 | \n199 | **The remaining work is the concrete wiring**: showing that the transfer-matrix\n200 | kernel (a product of plaquette Boltzmann factors, integrated over negative-time\n201 | links) is a PD function of the interface link variables — applying\n202 | `PositiveDefinite.integral` to integrate out the negative-time links from the\n203 | full Boltzmann factor (now proved PD by `boltzmannFactorPD`, item 15), and\n204 | finally applying `integralOperator_nonneg` to the resulting PD kernel.\n205 | \n206 | **The character-orthogonality path (new)**: the `characterOrthogonality` axiom\n207 | (Schur orthogonality, now in `PositiveDefinite.lean`) provides the key\n208 | ingredient to turn the character expansion of the Boltzmann factor into a\n209 | `|Fourier coefficient|²` decomposition of the reflection-positivity integral.\n210 | The path: (1) expand `exp(-β S_W) = ∑_λ a_λ χ_λ` (Peter–Weyl), (2) substitute\n211 | into `∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β S_W) dμ` (shown equivalent to the axiom by\n212 | `osG_thetaG_factorization`), (3) use reflection-invariance of Haar measure +\n213 | `characterOrthogonality` to rewrite each term as `|∫ f·χ_λ|² ≥ 0`, (4) sum\n214 | with `a_λ ≥ 0`.  Steps 1–2 are formalized; steps 3–4 are the remaining wiring.\n215 | \n216 | ### Precise analysis of the character-orthogonality path (2026-06-29 session)\n217 | \n218 | A detailed analysis of the character-orthogonality path was performed in the\n219 | 2026-06-29 session.  The key findings are:\n220 | \n221 | **The correct abstract lemma** is NOT at the level of the full Boltzmann\n222 | factor (where `χ_λ(θU) ≠ conj(χ_λ(U))` in general, so the naive\n223 | `|∫ f·χ_λ|²` decomposition fails).  It is at the level of the **transfer\n224 | matrix kernel**:\n225 | \n226 |     K_TM(u, U⁻) = exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁻(U⁻)/2 + S_int(u, U⁻)))\n227 | \n228 | If this kernel decomposes as\n229 | \n230 |     K_TM(u, U⁻) = ∑_λ a_λ · Φ_λ(u) · conj(Φ_λ(θ⁻⁰(U⁻, u⁰)))\n231 | \n232 | with `a_λ ≥ 0`, then (using `reflectLinkVariable_measurePreserving` for the\n233 | change of variables `U⁻ ↦ θ⁻⁰(U⁻, u⁰)`):\n234 | \n235 |     ∫∫ g(u)·g(θ⁻⁰(U⁻, u⁰))·K_TM(u, U⁻) dμ⁻(U⁻) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n236 |     = ∑_λ a_λ · ∫_{u⁰} |∫_{u⁺} g(u⁺, u⁰)·Φ_λ(u⁺, u⁰) dμ⁺(u⁺)|² dμ⁰(u⁰) ≥ 0\n237 | \n238 | The key steps are:\n239 | 1. **Kernel decomposition** (the hard part): the interface Boltzmann factor\n240 |    `exp(-β·S_int)` has a character expansion that separates into\n241 |    `u`-dependent and `U⁻`-dependent parts, related by reflection.\n242 | 2. **Change of variables**: `θ⁻⁰` maps `μ⁻` to `μ⁺` (by\n243 |    `reflectLinkVariable_measurePreserving`).\n244 | 3. **Algebra**: the integral becomes a sum of `|Fourier coefficients|² ≥ 0`.\n245 | \n246 | **The key obstruction** to formalizing step 1: the interface Boltzmann factor\n247 | is a **product of multiple interface plaquette factors**.  Each single\n248 | plaquette factor has a character expansion (by\n249 | `peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette`), but the **product** of multiple\n250 | expansions involves **products of characters of the same link variable**\n251 | (when a link appears in multiple plaquettes).  Reducing such a product to a\n252 | sum of single characters requires the **Clebsch–Gordan decomposition**\n253 | `χ_s(g)·χ_t(g) = ∑_w N^w_{st} χ_w(g)` with `N^w_{st} ≥ 0`, which is NOT\n254 | currently axiomatized.  The existing `peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette` axiom\n255 | bakes Clebsch–Gordan into the single-plaquette expansion, but does not\n256 | provide it as a separate tool for combining characters of the same group\n257 | element across different plaquettes.\n258 | \n259 | **The abstract lemma to formalize** (no new axioms needed): if a kernel\n260 | `K : X → Y → ℂ` has a finite separable decomposition\n261 | `K(x, y) = ∑_i a_i · Φ_i(x) · conj(Φ_i(θ y))` with `a_i ≥ 0` and `θ`\n262 | measure-preserving (`θ_*ν = μ`), then for real-valued `f`:\n263 | \n264 |     ∫∫ f(x) · f(θ y) · K(x, y) dν(y) dμ(x) = ∑_i a_i · |∫ f · Φ_i dμ|² ≥ 0\n265 | \n266 | This is a pure measure-theory lemma (uses only `MeasurePreserving` for the\n267 | change of variables, no group structure, no character orthogonality).  It is\n268 | the abstract scaffold that the concrete character expansion would plug into.\n269 | Formalizing it is the natural next step; it was sketched but not completed in\n270 | the 2026-06-29 session (the integrability bookkeeping for exchanging the\n271 | finite sum with the integral needs care).\n272 | \n273 | **Key obstruction**: the TM kernel\n274 | `(Tψ)(u) = ∫ ψ(θ⁻⁰(U⁻,u⁰))·exp(-β·(...)) dμ⁻(U⁻)` is NOT of the form\n275 | `φ(u⁻¹·v)` for a PD function `φ` on a group — the reflection map `θ⁻⁰` is a\n276 | geometric operation, not group multiplication.  While `PosInterfaceConfig` is a\n277 | product of SU(N)'s (hence a group), the kernel does not factor through the group\n278 | structure.  The Mercer framework (3–5 above) removes the *group-structure*\n279 | obstruction, but showing the TM kernel *is* Mercer-PD still requires the\n280 | Peter–Weyl character expansion to decompose the Boltzmann factor into separable\n281 | positive terms (approach (c)).  This is a fundamental mathematical gap, not just\n282 | formalization work.\n283 | \n284 | **Fundamental obstruction (resolved by Peter–Weyl, not by the abstract lemmas)**:\n285 | The function $(g_1, g_2, g_3, g_4) \\mapsto \\exp(c \\cdot \\operatorname{Re} \\operatorname{Tr}(g_1 g_2 g_3 g_4))$ is **NOT** positive-definite on $\\text{SU}(N)^4$ for $N \\ge 2$ as a naive composition, because even the simpler function $(g, h) \\mapsto \\operatorname{Tr}(gh)$ is NOT\n286 | positive-definite on $\\text{SU}(N) \\times \\text{SU}(N)$. A concrete counterexample for\n287 | $N=2$ uses the Pauli matrices $i\\sigma_1, i\\sigma_2, i\\sigma_3 \\in \\text{SU}(2)$:\n288 | \n289 | With $(g_a, h_a) = (i\\sigma_a, i\\sigma_a)$ for $a=1,2,3$ and $c_a = 1$, the kernel matrix\n290 | $K_{ab} = \\operatorname{Tr}(g_a^{-1} g_b h_a^{-1} h_b)$ has eigenvalues $-2, 4, 4$, giving\n291 | $\\sum c_a \\overline{c_b} K_{ab} = -6 < 0$.\n292 | \n293 | The character expansion (Peter–Weyl) resolves this: $\\exp(c \\cdot \\operatorname{Re} \\operatorname{Tr}(g_1g_2g_3g_4)) = \\sum_\\lambda a_\\lambda \\sum_{\\mu,\\nu,\\rho,\\sigma} C_{\\mu\\nu\\rho\\sigma}^\\lambda \\chi_\\mu(g_1) \\chi_\\nu(g_2) \\chi_\\rho(g_3) \\chi_\\sigma(g_4)$\n294 | where $a_\\lambda \\ge 0$, $C_{\\mu\\nu\\rho\\sigma}^\\lambda \\ge 0$ (Littlewood-Richardson), and each\n295 | $\\chi_\\mu(g_1) \\chi_\\nu(g_2) \\chi_\\rho(g_3) \\chi_\\sigma(g_4)$ is PD on $\\text{SU}(N)^4$ (product of PD functions on\n296 | different factors). This decomposition is captured by the `peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette` axiom and proved as `plaquetteBoltzmannPD` in `PeterWeyl.lean`.\n297 | \n298 | See `docs/found_issues.md` §3 for the full analysis.\n299 | \n300 | ### Precise analysis of why `character_expansion_positivity` does NOT directly apply (2026-07-02 session)\n301 | \n302 | A detailed analysis was performed of whether the abstract lemma\n303 | `character_expansion_positivity` (proved 2026-07-01, 0 sorries, 0 axioms) can\n304 | be directly wired into the lattice-gauge-theory setup to close\n305 | `transferMatrixPositivity_axiom`.  **The answer is no — three interconnected\n306 | obstructions prevent direct application.**  This section documents the precise\n307 | mathematical situation.\n308 | \n309 | **Recap of the abstract lemma.**  `character_expansion_positivity` states: if\n310 | `K : X → Y → ℂ` has a finite separable decomposition\n311 | `K(x, y) = ∑_i a_i · Φ_i(x) · conj(Φ_i(θ y))` with `a_i ≥ 0` and\n312 | `θ : Y → X` measure-preserving (`θ_*ν = μ`), then for real-valued `f`:\n313 | `∫∫ f(x) · f(θ y) · K(x, y) dν dμ = ∑_i a_i · ‖∫ f · Φ_i dμ‖² ≥ 0`.\n314 | \n315 | The lattice integral we need to show is ≥ 0 is (after `osG_thetaG_factorization`\n316 | and `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg`):\n317 | \n318 |     I = ∫_u ∫_{U⁻} g(u) · g(θ⁻⁰(U⁻, u⁰)) · K_TM(u, U⁻) dμ⁻(U⁻) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n319 | \n320 | where `u = (u⁺, u⁰)` is a `PosInterfaceConfig` (positive + interface links),\n321 | `U⁻` is a negative config, `θ⁻⁰(U⁻, u⁰)` is the reflection from negative +\n322 | interface to positive + interface, and `K_TM` is the transfer-matrix kernel.\n323 | \n324 | **Obstruction 1: `θ⁻⁰` depends on both `x` and `y`.**  The lemma requires\n325 | `θ : Y → X` (a function of `y` only).  But `θ⁻⁰(U⁻, u⁰)` depends on `u⁰`,\n326 | which is part of `x = u = (u⁺, u⁰)`.  So `θ` is NOT a function of `y` only —\n327 | it depends on `x` too.  The lemma's proof uses the change of variables\n\n[... 199 lines omitted ...]\n\n527 | \n528 | **Key insight for future sessions**: the `character_expansion_positivity`\n529 | lemma, while a valuable abstract result, is NOT the right scaffold for the\n530 | lattice case.  The correct approach is the operator-theoretic `T = B* B`\n531 | argument, which requires CG and the full plaquette combinatorics.  The\n532 | abstract lemma's requirement that `θ : Y → X` be a function of `y` only (and\n533 | measure-preserving to the FULL measure on `X`) is fundamentally incompatible\n534 | with the lattice setup's shared interface structure and the `σ` reflection on\n535 | interface time-like links.\n536 | \n537 | ### ✅ Clean factorization PROVEN (2026-06-29)\n538 | \n539 | The lemma `osG_thetaG_factorization` (in `ReflectionPositivity.lean`, 0 sorries,\n540 | 0 axioms) proves the purely algebraic identity:\n541 | \n542 | $$\\texttt{osG}(U) \\cdot \\texttt{osG}(\\theta U) = f(U) \\cdot f(\\theta U) \\cdot \\exp(-\\beta S_W(U))$$\n543 | \n544 | This follows from the reflection symmetries $S^+_{OS}(\\theta U) = S^-_{OS}(U)$ and\n545 | $S^0_{OS}(\\theta U) = S^0_{OS}(U)$, together with the action decomposition\n546 | $S_W = S^+_{OS} + S^-_{OS} + S^0_{OS}$.  No support hypothesis on $f$ is needed.\n547 | \n548 | This shows that `transferMatrixPositivity_axiom` is equivalent to:\n549 | \n550 | $$\\int f(U) \\cdot f(\\theta U) \\cdot \\exp(-\\beta S_W(U)) \\, d\\mu_0(U) \\ge 0$$\n551 | \n552 | where $\\exp(-\\beta S_W)$ is the full Boltzmann factor, proved positive-definite on\n553 | the full link-variable group by `boltzmannFactorPD` (item 15).\n554 | \n555 | ### ❌ Why PD-ness of the Boltzmann factor is NOT sufficient\n556 | \n557 | The integral $\\int f(U) \\cdot f(\\theta U) \\cdot \\exp(-\\beta S_W(U)) \\, d\\mu_0(U)$\n558 | is **NOT** the standard positive-definite quadratic form\n559 | \n560 | $$\\iint f(g)\\,\\overline{f(h)}\\,K(g^{-1} h)\\,d\\mu(g)\\,d\\mu(h) \\ge 0$$\n561 | \n562 | which follows from PD-ness of $K$ on a group (proven as\n563 | `PositiveDefinite.integralOperator_nonneg`).  Instead, it is a **single** integral\n564 | $\\int f(g)\\,f(\\theta g)\\,K(g)\\,d\\mu(g)$ with:\n565 | \n566 | 1. The **geometric reflection** $\\theta$ (not group multiplication or inversion).\n567 | 2. $K$ evaluated at $g$ (not $g^{-1} h$ — there is no \"second variable\" $h$).\n568 | 3. $f(\\theta g)$ (not $\\overline{f(h)}$ — no complex conjugation, and the argument\n569 |    is $\\theta g$, not $h$).\n570 | \n571 | PD-ness of $K = \\exp(-\\beta S_W)$ on the group does **not** imply this integral is\n572 | non-negative.  The Peter–Weyl character expansion of $K$ and character\n573 | orthogonality are needed to decompose the integrand into $|\\text{Fourier\n574 | coefficients}|^2$, which are manifestly non-negative.  This is the fundamental\n575 | mathematical gap that remains.\n576 | \n577 | ## Why This Is Non-Trivial\n578 | \n579 | The integrand $G(U) \\cdot G(\\theta U)$ is **not pointwise non-negative**. The\n580 | non-negativity holds only after integration. This is the essence of reflection\n581 | positivity: it is a global property of the measure, not a pointwise one.\n582 | \n583 | ## Mathematical Proof (Osterwalder-Seiler 1979, §3)\n584 | \n585 | ### Step 1: Measure factorization\n586 | \n587 | The links are partitioned into three sets:\n588 | - $L^+$: links with positive signed time\n589 | - $L^-$: links with negative signed time\n590 | - $L^0$: links at the time interface (signed time = 0)\n591 | \n592 | The product Haar measure factorizes:\n593 | $$\\mu_0 = \\mu^+ \\otimes \\mu^- \\otimes \\mu^0$$\n594 | \n595 | ### Step 2: Transfer matrix\n596 | \n597 | Define the transfer matrix $T$ acting on $L^2(\\mu^+ \\otimes \\mu^0)$ by:\n598 | \n599 | $$(T\\psi)(U^+, U^0) = \\int_{U^-} \\psi(U^-, U^0) \\cdot K(U^+, U^-, U^0) \\, d\\mu^-(U^-)$$\n600 | \n601 | where $K(U^+, U^-, U^0) = \\exp(-\\beta \\cdot S^0_{OS}(U^+, U^-, U^0))$ is the\n602 | interface Boltzmann factor.\n603 | \n604 | ### Step 3: Key identity\n605 | \n606 | $$\\int G(U) G(\\theta U) \\, d\\mu_0 = \\langle G^+, T G^+ \\rangle_{L^2(\\mu^+ \\otimes \\mu^0)}$$\n607 | \n608 | where $G^+$ is the restriction of $G$ to $L^+ \\cup L^0$.\n609 | \n610 | ### Step 4: Positivity of $T$\n611 | \n612 | The transfer matrix $T$ is a **positive operator** (self-adjoint with non-negative\n613 | spectrum). This follows from the Peter-Weyl theorem on $\\text{SU}(N)$:\n614 | \n615 | 1. The kernel $K(U^+, U^-, U^0)$ is a product over interface plaquettes:\n616 |    $$K = \\prod_{\\text{interface plaquettes } p} \\exp\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{N} \\operatorname{Re} \\operatorname{Tr}(U_{\\partial p})\\right)$$\n617 | \n618 | 2. For a single plaquette, the function $\\exp(c \\operatorname{Re} \\operatorname{Tr}(g))$ on\n619 |    $\\text{SU}(N)$ can be expanded in characters (representations):\n620 |    $$\\exp(c \\operatorname{Re} \\operatorname{Tr}(g)) = \\sum_{\\lambda} a_\\lambda(c) \\chi_\\lambda(g)$$\n621 |    with $a_\\lambda(c) \\ge 0$ for $c > 0$.\n622 | \n623 | 3. The characters $\\chi_\\lambda$ are positive-definite functions on $\\text{SU}(N)$,\n624 |    and the tensor product of positive-definite functions is positive-definite.\n625 | \n626 | 4. By the Peter-Weyl theorem, the matrix elements of irreducible representations\n627 |    form an orthonormal basis of $L^2(\\text{SU}(N))$, and any positive-definite\n628 |    function expands with non-negative coefficients in this basis.\n629 | \n630 | 5. Therefore $K$ is a positive-definite kernel, $T$ is a positive operator,\n631 |    and $\\langle G^+, T G^+ \\rangle \\ge 0$.\n632 | \n633 | ## Required Mathlib Infrastructure\n634 | \n635 | ### 1. Peter-Weyl Theorem for $\\text{SU}(N)$\n636 | \n637 | **Not yet in Mathlib.** The following pieces are needed:\n638 | \n639 | | Piece | Status in Mathlib | Notes |\n640 | |-------|-------------------|-------|\n641 | | Compact Lie group $\\text{SU}(N)$ | Done (`SpecialUnitary.lean`) | Topological group, compactness, Haar measure |\n642 | | Representation theory of compact groups | Partial | `RepresentationTheory/` covers finite groups |\n643 | | Unitary irreps of $\\text{SU}(N)$ | Not available | Requires highest weight theory |\n644 | | Peter-Weyl theorem | Not available | $L^2(G) \\cong \\bigoplus_\\lambda V_\\lambda \\otimes V_\\lambda^*$ |\n645 | | Characters of irreps | Partial (finite groups) | `RepresentationTheory/Character` |\n646 | | Orthogonality of characters | Partial (finite groups) | |\n647 | | Positive-definite functions on groups | Not available | |\n648 | | Bochner theorem for compact groups | Not available | |\n649 | \n650 | ### 2. Transfer Matrix Framework\n651 | \n652 | **Nothing in Mathlib.** The following need to be built:\n653 | \n654 | | Component | Description |\n655 | |-----------|-------------|\n656 | | $L^2$ space on $\\text{SU}(N)^n$ | Built on existing measure theory |\n657 | | Integral operators on $L^2$ | As kernel operators |\n658 | | Positivity of operators | Spectral theory of bounded operators |\n659 | | Product measures and Fubini | Available in Mathlib |\n660 | \n661 | ### 3. Character Expansion of Wilson Action\n662 | \n663 | | Component | Description |\n664 | |-----------|-------------|\n665 | | Expansion of $\\exp(c \\operatorname{Re} \\operatorname{Tr}(g))$ in characters | Requires representation theory |\n666 | | Positivity of expansion coefficients | Known result (Osterwalder-Seiler) |\n667 | | Factorization over plaquettes | Already done algebraically |\n668 | \n669 | ## Alternative Approaches\n670 | \n671 | ### A. Product of PD on different factors (✅ PROVEN)\n672 | \n673 | The lemma `PositiveDefinite.prod` (in `PositiveDefinite.lean`) proves that if\n674 | `φ : G → ℂ` is positive-definite and `ψ : H → ℂ` is positive-definite, then\n675 | `(g, h) ↦ φ(g) * ψ(h)` is positive-definite on `G × H`.\n676 | \n677 | **Proof**: For any finset `s ⊂ G × H` and coefficients `c`, define matrices\n678 | `A_{ij} = φ(g_i⁻¹ g_j)` and `B_{ij} = ψ(h_i⁻¹ h_j)`. Both are PSD by the\n679 | grouping argument (`PositiveDefinite.sum_nonneg_of_map`), which handles\n680 | non-injective maps `f : α → G` by regrouping the quadratic form by fibers.\n681 | By the Schur product theorem (`Matrix.PosSemidef.hadamard`), `A ⊙ B` is PSD,\n682 | and the quadratic form of `A ⊙ B` equals the PD sum for the product function.\n683 | \n684 | This is one of the three ingredients needed for the character expansion approach\n685 | (see `docs/found_issues.md` §3). The remaining ingredients are:\n686 | 1. ✅ Product of PD on different factors — **PROVEN** (`PositiveDefinite.prod`)\n687 | 2. ❌ Peter-Weyl theorem: `exp(c·Re Tr(g)) = ∑_λ a_λ χ_λ(g)` with `a_λ ≥ 0`\n688 | 3. ❌ Clebsch-Gordan decomposition: `χ_λ(gh) = ∑_{μ,ν} N^λ_{μν} χ_μ(g) χ_ν(h)`\n689 | \n690 | ### B. Heat Kernel Approach\n691 | \n692 | Instead of the character expansion, one could use the heat kernel on $\\text{SU}(N)$:\n693 | \n694 | $$\\exp\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{N} \\operatorname{Re} \\operatorname{Tr}(g)\\right) = \\int_{\\mathfrak{su}(N)} e^{\\langle X, \\log g \\rangle} \\, d\\mu_\\beta(X)$$\n695 | \n696 | where $\\mu_\\beta$ is a Gaussian measure on the Lie algebra $\\mathfrak{su}(N)$.\n697 | This represents the Boltzmann factor as the Fourier transform of a positive measure,\n698 | which is automatically positive-definite.\n699 | \n700 | **Status in Mathlib**: \n701 | - Gaussian measures: Available (`Mathlib/MeasureTheory/`)\n702 | - Lie algebra $\\mathfrak{su}(N)$: Defined as `LieAlgebraSU` in `SpecialUnitary.lean`\n703 | - Matrix exponential: Not available\n704 | - Fourier transform on Lie groups: Not available\n705 | \n706 | ### B. Reflection Positivity per Plaquette\n707 | \n708 | One could attempt to prove that each individual plaquette contribution is\n709 | reflection-positive, and then use the fact that the product of reflection-positive\n710 | functions is reflection-positive. This would reduce the problem to proving\n711 | reflection positivity for a single plaquette.\n712 | \n713 | **Status**: This is essentially equivalent to the character expansion approach,\n714 | since a single plaquette is the fundamental building block.\n715 | \n716 | ### C. Finite Lattice Spectral Argument\n717 | \n718 | For a strictly finite lattice, the transfer matrix is a finite-rank operator\n719 | (acting on a finite-dimensional space if we discretize the group... but SU(N) is\n720 | continuous, so still infinite-dimensional).\n721 | \n722 | ### Current Status\n723 | \n724 | The corrected transfer matrix framework has been implemented in `TransferMatrix.lean`:\n725 | \n726 | - **`reflectToPosInterface`**: The map θ⁻⁰ from negative+interface to positive+interface.\n727 | - **`g_posInterface`**: The restricted function `g(u) = f(u)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(u)/2)`.\n728 | - **`transferMatrixCorrect`**: The correct transfer matrix with reflection kernel.\n729 | - **`integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg`**: ✅ PROVEN (0 sorries). The key identity.\n730 | - **`transferMatrixCorrect_positive`**: ❌ BLOCKED — requires Peter-Weyl theorem.\n731 |   See `docs/found_issues.md` §3 for the precise obstruction (counterexample showing\n732 |   `Tr(gh)` is not PD on `SU(N) × SU(N)`).\n733 | - **`integral_G_thetaG_nonneg'`**: Depends on the above.\n734 | \n735 | ### Remaining Steps:\n736 | \n737 | ### Short-term (measure-theoretic bookkeeping, no representation theory):\n738 | \n739 | 1. **Prove `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg`**: ✅ DONE. The identity\n740 |    ∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀ = ∫ g(u)·(T g)(u) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n741 |    is fully proven using `measure_factorization'`, the action reflection lemmas, Haar measure\n742 |    invariance under reflection, and the definition of `transferMatrixCorrect`.\n743 |    This is purely measure-theoretic — no representation theory is needed.\n744 | \n745 | ### Medium-term (requires representation theory — BLOCKED):\n746 | \n747 | 2. **Prove `transferMatrixCorrect_positive`**: ❌ BLOCKED. The transfer matrix T is positive\n748 |    iff its kernel is a positive-definite function on the link-variable group. The kernel is a\n749 |    product of plaquette factors exp(c·Re Tr(U_∂p)), and each factor must be PD on SU(N)^4.\n750 | \n751 |    **Obstruction**: The function (g,h) ↦ Tr(gh) is NOT positive-definite on SU(N) × SU(N)\n752 |    for N ≥ 2 (counterexample with Pauli matrices, see `docs/found_issues.md` §3). The\n753 |    multiplication map SU(N)^k → SU(N) is NOT a group homomorphism for non-abelian groups,\n754 |    so composing a PD function with it does NOT preserve PD-ness.\n755 | \n756 |    **What's needed**: The character expansion (Peter-Weyl theorem) decomposes\n757 |    exp(c·Re Tr(g₁g₂g₃g₄)) into a sum of products χ_μ(g₁)·χ_ν(g₂)·χ_ρ(g₃)·χ_σ(g₄)\n758 |    with non-negative coefficients, where each factor is a PD function on SU(N). This\n759 |    requires:\n760 |    - Peter-Weyl theorem for compact Lie groups (NOT in Mathlib)\n761 |    - Clebsch-Gordan / Littlewood-Richardson decomposition (NOT in Mathlib)\n762 |    - Character expansion of exp(c·Re Tr(g)) with non-negative coefficients (NOT in Mathlib)\n763 | \n764 | 3. **Prove `exp_reTrace_positiveDefinite`** (`PositiveDefinite.lean`): ✅ DONE.\n765 |    exp(c Re Tr(g)) is positive-definite on SU(N) for c ≥ 0. This is the core analytic fact\n766 |    needed for (2), but it is NOT SUFFICIENT by itself — the composition with the plaquette\n767 |    product map does not preserve PD-ness for non-abelian groups.\n768 | \n769 | ### Long-term (upstream to Mathlib):\n770 | \n771 | 1. **Peter-Weyl theorem** for compact Lie groups\n772 | 2. **Character theory** for compact (not just finite) groups\n773 | 3. **Positive-definite functions** and Bochner's theorem for compact groups\n774 | \n775 | 1. K. Osterwalder, E. Seiler, \"Gauge Field Theories on a Lattice\"\n776 |    (Ann. Phys. 110, 1978, pp 440–471), §3.\n777 |    - The original proof of reflection positivity for lattice gauge theories.\n778 |    - Uses the character expansion and Peter-Weyl theorem.\n779 | \n780 | 2. M. Lüscher, \"General Proof of Osterwalder-Schrader Positivity for the\n781 |    Wilson Action\" (Commun. Math. Phys. 113, 1987, pp 311–325).\n782 |    - Alternative proof using explicit transfer matrix construction.\n783 |    - Does not require fermions.\n784 | \n785 | 3. P. Menotti, A. Pelissetto, \"General Proof of Osterwalder-Schrader Positivity\n786 |    for the Wilson Action\" (Commun. Math. Phys. 113, 1987, pp 369–373).\n787 |    - Extension to reflections about planes containing sites.\n788 | \n789 | 4. J. Glimm, A. Jaffe, \"Quantum Physics\" (2nd ed.), §6.1.\n790 |    - Textbook treatment of reflection positivity.\n791 | \n792 | 5. M. Salmhofer, \"Construction of a Higgs field\" (thesis, 1990).\n793 |    - Detailed treatment of the transfer matrix for lattice gauge theories.\n794 | \n795 | 6. T. Bröcker, T. tom Dieck, \"Representations of Compact Lie Groups\"\n796 |    (Springer, 1985).\n797 |    - Standard reference for the Peter-Weyl theorem.\n\n---\npath:C:\\github\\YangMills\\docs\\transfer_matrix_positivity_design.md\n-- 331 lines, 17861 bytes --\n  1 | # Design Document: Closing `transferMatrixPositivity_axiom` via T = B*·B\n  2 | \n  3 | **Date**: 2026-07-30\n  4 | **Status**: Design phase (sub-step (a) in progress)\n  5 | **Goal**: Reduce axiom count from 6 to 5 by proving `transferMatrixPositivity_axiom`\n  6 | from `peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette` + `characterOrthogonality`.\n  7 | \n  8 | ## 1. Overview\n  9 | \n 10 | The axiom `transferMatrixPositivity_axiom` (ReflectionPositivity.lean, line ~1366)\n 11 | asserts that for `f` depending only on positive+interface links:\n 12 | \n 13 |     ∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀ ≥ 0\n 14 | \n 15 | where `G = osG` is the Osterwalder-Seiler Boltzmann factor and `θ` is time-reflection.\n 16 | This is equivalent (via `osG_thetaG_factorization` and `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg`)\n 17 | to the positivity of the transfer matrix `T`:\n 18 | \n 19 |     ⟨g, Tg⟩_{L²(μ⁺⁰)} ≥ 0\n 20 | \n 21 | The proof uses the **operator-theoretic T = B*·B argument**: the character expansion\n 22 | of the interface Boltzmann factor defines an operator `B` (Fourier coefficient\n 23 | extraction), and `T = B*·B` gives `⟨g, Tg⟩ = ‖Bg‖² ≥ 0`.\n 24 | \n 25 | ## 2. Mathematical Structure\n 26 | \n 27 | ### 2.1 The transfer matrix\n 28 | \n 29 | After the change of variables `V⁺ = reflect(U⁻)` (using\n 30 | `reflectLinkVariable_measurePreserving` and `S⁻(U⁻) = S⁺(reflect(U⁻))`):\n 31 | \n 32 |     (Tg)(u) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺)/2 + S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)))) dμ⁺(V⁺)\n 33 | \n 34 | where:\n 35 | - `u = (U⁺, u⁰)` is a `PosInterfaceConfig` (positive + interface links)\n 36 | - `σ` is the interface reflection: inverts time-like interface links, keeps spatial\n 37 | - `S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺))` is the interface action with negative part = reflect(V⁺)\n 38 | \n 39 | ### 2.2 The interface plaquettes\n 40 | \n 41 | Interface plaquettes are those NOT purely positive and NOT purely negative (i.e.,\n 42 | corners straddle the time interface at signed time 0). They involve links from three\n 43 | regions:\n 44 | \n 45 | | Type | Base site time | Directions | Links involved |\n 46 | |------|---------------|------------|----------------|\n 47 | | A | t=0 | (0, ν) spatial | time-0 (interface) + time-1 (positive) |\n 48 | | B | t=T-1 (signed -1) | (0, ν) spatial | time-T-1 (negative) + time-0 (interface) |\n 49 | | C | t=0 | (μ, ν) both spatial | time-0 only (interface) |\n 50 | \n 51 | After change of variables V⁺ = reflect(U⁻), type B plaquettes' negative links\n 52 | (time T-1) become V⁺ links (time 1), with **inversion for time-like links**\n 53 | (since reflection inverts time-like links).\n 54 | \n 55 | ### 2.3 The plaquette product and inversion\n 56 | \n 57 | Each plaquette (n, μ, ν) has product:\n 58 |     U(n,μ) · U(n+e_μ,ν) · U(n+e_μ+e_ν,μ)⁻¹ · U(n+e_ν,ν)⁻¹\n 59 | \n 60 | The 3rd and 4th links are **inverted**. By `repCharacter_inv`:\n 61 |     χ(g⁻¹) = conj(χ(g)) = χ_{dual}(g)  (via the dual map in the axiom)\n 62 | \n 63 | So the character expansion of a plaquette factor with inverted links produces\n 64 | **conjugated characters** for the 3rd and 4th links.\n 65 | \n 66 | ### 2.4 The reflection symmetry of S_int\n 67 | \n 68 | The key symmetry (proved as `interface_action_reflection_symmetric_os_periodic`):\n 69 |     S_int(θU) = S_int(U)\n 70 | \n 71 | Since θ reflects ALL links (positive ↔ negative, interface → interface with σ), this\n 72 | gives:\n 73 |     S_int(U⁺, u⁰, U⁻) = S_int(reflect(U⁻), σ(u⁰), reflect(U⁺))\n 74 | \n 75 | After change of variables U⁻ = reflect(V⁺):\n 76 |     S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)) = S_int(V⁺, σ(u⁰), reflect(U⁺))\n 77 | \n 78 | This means the interface Boltzmann factor K(U⁺, u⁰, V⁺) = exp(-β·S_int(...)) satisfies:\n 79 |     K(U⁺, u⁰, V⁺) = K(V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺)\n 80 | \n 81 | ### 2.5 The σ reflection on interface links\n 82 | \n 83 | For interface sites (signed time = 0), θn = n (since -0 = 0 in ZMod T). So on\n 84 | interface links:\n 85 | - Time-like (μ=0): (θU)(n, 0) = U(n, 0)⁻¹ — **inverted**\n 86 | - Spatial (μ≠0): (θU)(n, μ) = U(n, μ) — **unchanged**\n 87 | \n 88 | This σ reflection is the source of obstruction 3 (gap_analysis.md): it prevents the\n 89 | direct `character_expansion_positivity` approach, because the integral becomes\n 90 | `⟨A_i, A_i ∘ σ⟩` (inner product with σ-composed), not `‖A_i‖²`.\n 91 | \n 92 | ### 2.6 The T = B*·B resolution\n 93 | \n 94 | The OS proof resolves this by constructing `B` via the character expansion and showing\n 95 | `T = B*·B`. The key steps (from gap_analysis.md lines 383-394):\n 96 | \n 97 | 1. **Expand each interface plaquette factor** in characters (Peter-Weyl), with\n 98 |    conjugation for inverted links (via the dual map).\n 99 | 2. **For interface time-like links** appearing in both a plaquette `p` and its\n100 |    reflection `θp`, the product `χ_s(g) · conj(χ_t(g))` arises. This is a matrix\n101 |    coefficient of `ρ_s ⊗ ρ_t*`, decomposable via CG into `∑_w N^w χ_w(g)` with\n102 |    `N^w ≥ 0` (using `charProduct_mixed_finset_decomp'`).\n103 | 3. **For links appearing in multiple plaquettes**, CG reduces products of characters\n104 |    of the same link to single characters (using `charProduct_mixed_link_separable_decomp`).\n105 | 4. The resulting decomposition defines `B` (Fourier coefficient extraction), and\n106 |    `T = B*·B` gives `⟨g, Tg⟩ = ‖Bg‖² ≥ 0`.\n107 | \n108 | ## 3. Formalization Plan\n109 | \n110 | ### Step (a): Separable decomposition of the interface Boltzmann factor\n111 | \n112 | **Goal**: Show that the product of interface plaquette Boltzmann factors has a\n113 | separable character decomposition with non-negative coefficients.\n114 | \n115 | **Abstract lemma** (`plaquette_product_separable_decomp` in PeterWeyl.lean):\n116 | \n117 | Given the Peter-Weyl axiom data (ι, ρ, coeff, cg, dual, etc.), a finite type `P` of\n118 | plaquettes, a finite type `L` of links, a link assignment `links : P → Fin 4 → L`\n119 | (surjective: every link appears in ≥1 plaquette), and link variables `g : L → SU N`:\n120 | \n121 |     ∏_{p ∈ P} exp(c · Re Tr(g₁·g₂·g₃⁻¹·g₄⁻¹))\n122 |       = ∑_{w : L → ι} F(w) · ∏_{l ∈ L} χ_{w(l)}(g_l)\n123 | \n124 | where `g_i = g(links p i)`, `F(w) ≥ 0`, and the 3rd/4th links are inverted.\n125 | \n126 | **Status (2026-07-30): PROVED.** The lemma `plaquette_product_separable_decomp`\n127 | is fully proved in `PeterWeyl.lean` with 0 sorries and 0 custom axioms\n128 | (`#print axioms` confirms only `propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`).\n129 | All 5 stages of the proof structure below are implemented.\n130 | \n131 | **Proof structure**:\n132 | 1. For each plaquette `p`, apply `hexp4` with `(g₁, g₂, g₃⁻¹, g₄⁻¹)` and use\n133 |    `repCharacter_inv` / `hdual` to get `conj(χ)` for inverted links.\n134 | 2. Product of sums = sum of products (`Fintype.prod_sum`): expand the product of\n135 |    per-plaquette character sums into a sum over `α : P → ι⁵` of products.\n136 | 3. For each `α`, the product is `∏_p ∏_j (if j≥2 then conj(χ) else χ)`. Regroup by\n137 |    link: `∏_l ∏_{(p,j): links p j = l} (...)`.\n138 | 4. Apply `charProduct_mixed_link_separable_decomp` to get a per-`α` separable\n139 |    decomposition `∑_w F_α(w) · ∏_l χ_{w(l)}(g_l)` with `F_α(w) ≥ 0`.\n140 | 5. Sum over `α`: `∑_α (∏_p coeff(α p)) · ∑_w F_α(w) · ∏_l χ_{w(l)}(g_l)`.\n141 | 6. Exchange sums: `∑_w (∑_α (∏_p coeff(α p)) · F_α(w)) · ∏_l χ_{w(l)}(g_l)`.\n142 | 7. The coefficient `G(w) = ∑_α (∏_p coeff(α p)) · F_α(w) ≥ 0` (sum of products of\n143 |    non-negative reals).\n144 | \n145 | **Key challenges**:\n146 | - **Step 1**: Substituting `g₃⁻¹, g₄⁻¹` into `hexp4` and converting `χ(g⁻¹)` to\n147 |   `conj(χ(g))` via `repCharacter_inv` (or to `χ_{dual}(g)` via `hdual`).\n148 | - **Step 3**: Regrouping a product indexed by `P × Fin 4` into a product grouped by\n149 |   link (partition of `P × Fin 4` by the `links` map). This requires a\n150 |   \"product over partition\" lemma.\n151 | - **Step 4**: Setting up the appearance structure `(A, S, charIdx, isConj)` for\n152 |   `charProduct_mixed_link_separable_decomp`, where `A = P × Fin 4`,\n153 |   `S l = {(p,j) : links p j = l}`, `charIdx` depends on `α`, and `isConj (p,j) = (j ≥ 2)`.\n154 | - **Step 6**: Exchanging the `α` and `w` sums (`Fintype.sum_comm`).\n155 | \n156 | **Status**: In progress. The abstract lemma will be stated and proved in\n157 | `PeterWeyl.lean`, taking the Peter-Weyl axiom data as explicit parameters (following\n158 | the pattern of `charProduct_mixed_link_separable_decomp`).\n159 | \n160 | ### Step (b): Change of variables in the transfer-matrix integral\n161 | \n162 | **Goal**: After obtaining the separable decomposition of the interface Boltzmann\n163 | factor, change variables in the transfer-matrix integral to express `⟨g, Tg⟩` in\n164 | terms of the separable decomposition.\n165 | \n166 | **Status (2026-07-30 session 7): IN PROGRESS.** The key measure-theoretic\n167 | ingredient and the involution property are now PROVED:\n168 | \n169 | - `reflectLinkVariable_measurePreserving_between` (LatticeMeasure.lean, ~line 383):\n170 |   The reflection maps link configs on `sourceSites` to link configs on `targetSites`\n171 |   in a measure-preserving way, when `reflectSite` gives a bijection between the two\n172 |   site sets. This generalizes `reflectLinkVariable_measurePreserving` (which is the\n173 |   special case `sourceSites = targetSites`). **0 sorries, 0 custom axioms**\n174 |   (verified by `#print axioms`: only `propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`).\n175 |   The proof is the same two-step composition as `reflectLinkVariable_measurePreserving`:\n176 |   (1) index bijection via `measurePreserving_piCongrLeft`, (2) componentwise inversion\n177 |   via `measurePreserving_pi` + `haarMeasure_inv_invariant`.\n178 | \n179 | - `reflectToPosInterface_involution` (TransferMatrix.lean, ~line 1751): **PROVED**\n180 |   (session 7). Shows that\n181 |   `reflectToPosInterface(reflectPosToNeg(V⁺), u⁰) = mergePosInterface(V⁺, σ(u⁰))` —\n182 |   i.e., reflecting the negative config `reflectPosToNeg(V⁺)` back to the\n183 |   positive+interface region recovers `V⁺` on positive links and `σ(u⁰)` on interface\n184 |   links. **0 sorries, 0 custom axioms** (verified by `#print axioms`: only `propext`,\n185 |   `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`). The proof proceeds by extensionality and case\n186 |   analysis (positive sites vs interface sites), using `ReflectSite.involution` for\n187 |   the double reflection and helper lemmas about `reflectSite` mapping between site\n188 |   sets (added to ReflectionPositivity.lean).\n189 | \n190 | **New definitions** (TransferMatrix.lean):\n191 | - `reflectPosToNeg`: maps positive configs → negative configs via reflection. This is\n192 |   the inverse of the change-of-variables map `U⁻ ↦ V⁺ = reflect(U⁻)`.\n193 | - `sigmaInterface`: the σ reflection on interface configs (inverts time-like links,\n194 |   keeps spatial), defined as `restrictLinkVariable interfaceSites (reflectLinkVariable\n195 |   (extendLinkVariable interfaceSites U_zero))`.\n196 | \n197 | **New helper lemmas** (ReflectionPositivity.lean, ~line 206):\n198 | - `signedTime_reflectSite`: `signedTime(reflectSite(n)) = -signedTime(n)`.\n199 | - `reflectSite_mem_negative_of_positive`: positive → negative.\n200 | - `reflectSite_mem_interface_of_interface`: interface → interface.\n201 | - `reflectSite_not_mem_positive_of_interface`, `reflectSite_not_mem_negative_of_interface`:\n202 |   interface sites don't map to positive/negative.\n203 | - `reflectSite_not_mem_positive_of_positive`: positive sites don't map to positive.\n204 | - `reflectSite_mem_positive_of_negative`: negative → positive.\n205 | - `reflectSite_not_mem_negative_of_negative`: negative sites don't map to negative.\n206 | \n207 | **New supporting lemmas** (TransferMatrix.lean, ~line 1815):\n208 | - `restrictLinkVariable_negative_extendToFullConfig`: restricting\n209 |   `extendToFullConfig U_minus u` to negative sites recovers `U_minus`.\n210 | - `restrictPosInterface_extendToFullConfig`: restricting\n211 |   `extendToFullConfig U_minus u` to positive+interface sites recovers `u`.\n212 | - `reflect_extendToFullConfig_posInterface`: the positive+interface restriction\n213 |   of `reflectLinkVariable(extendToFullConfig(reflectPosToNeg V⁺, u))` equals\n214 |   `mergePosInterface V⁺ (σ(restrictToInterface u))`. This combines\n215 |   `reflectToPosInterface_eq_restrict` with `reflectToPosInterface_involution` and\n216 |   is the key lemma for rewriting `S⁺` under the change of variables.\n217 |   All **0 sorries, 0 custom axioms** (verified by `#print axioms`: only `propext`,\n218 |   `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`).\n219 | \n220 | **Remaining sub-step for (b)**:\n221 | 1. **transferMatrix_change_of_variables** — **PROVED (session 7)**. The\n222 |    integral-level change of variables is complete. The lemma\n223 |    `transferMatrix_change_of_variables` (TransferMatrix.lean, ~line 1994) shows\n224 |    `transferMatrixCorrect = transferMatrixReflected`, applying\n225 |    `reflectLinkVariable_measurePreserving_between` (measure-preserving from μ⁻ to\n226 |    μ⁺) via `integral_map` together with the pointwise identity\n227 |    `transferMatrix_integrand_change_of_variables`. **0 sorries, 0 custom axioms**\n228 |    (verified by `#print axioms`: only `propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`).\n229 | \n230 | **Step (b) is COMPLETE.** The change of variables `U⁻ ↦ V⁺ = reflect(U⁻)` is\n231 | fully formalized at both the pointwise and integral levels. The transfer matrix\n232 | can now be written in the \"reflected\" form:\n233 | ```\n234 | (Tψ)(u) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫_{V⁺} ψ(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) ·\n235 |           exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2 + S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)))) dμ⁺(V⁺)\n236 | ```\n237 | where the negative-time integral has been replaced by a positive-time integral.\n238 | \n239 | **Key ingredients** (all proved):\n240 | - `reflectLinkVariable_measurePreserving_between` (proved this session): the\n241 |   reflection is measure-preserving from μ⁻ to μ⁺.\n242 | - `haarMeasure_inv_invariant` (proved): Haar measure is invariant under inversion.\n243 | - `neg_action_reflection_os_periodic` (proved): S⁻(U⁻) = S⁺(reflect(U⁻)).\n244 | - `reflection_involution` (proved): θ(θU) = U.\n245 | - The reflection symmetry `S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)) = S_int(V⁺, σ(u⁰), reflect(U⁺))`\n246 |   (from `interface_action_reflection_symmetric_os_periodic`).\n247 | \n248 | ### Step (c): Use CG with dual representations + character orthogonality\n249 | \n250 | **Goal**: Use the CG decomposition (with dual representations, via the dual map) to\n251 | combine reflected characters with unreflected ones, then use `characterOrthogonality`\n252 | to evaluate the integrals and obtain `∑_w a_w · |Fourier coefficient|² ≥ 0`.\n253 | \n254 | **Key ingredients**:\n255 | - `charProduct_mixed_finset_decomp'` (proved): mixed-conjugation CG decomposition.\n256 | - `charProduct_mixed_link_separable_decomp` (proved): per-term separable decomposition\n257 |   with mixed conjugation.\n258 | - `characterOrthogonality` (axiom): Schur orthogonality for irreducible characters.\n259 | \n260 | ### Step (d): Conclude T = B*·B and ⟨g, Tg⟩ = ‖Bg‖² ≥ 0\n261 | \n262 | **Goal**: Assemble the pieces to show `T = B*·B` and conclude positivity, closing\n263 | `transferMatrixPositivity_axiom`.\n264 | \n265 | ## 4. Key Insight: The σ Reflection and Interface Time-Like Links\n266 | \n267 | The fundamental difficulty is the **σ reflection on interface time-like links**.\n268 | When a time-like interface link `g` appears in both the original and reflected\n269 | plaquette factors, the product `χ_s(g) · conj(χ_t(g))` arises. This is NOT\n270 | `|χ(g)|² = χ(g) · conj(χ(g))` (which would be non-negative), but rather a product of\n271 | two different characters, which is complex in general.\n272 | \n273 | The resolution: `χ_s(g) · conj(χ_t(g))` is a matrix coefficient of `ρ_s ⊗ ρ_t*`,\n274 | which decomposes via CG (with dual representations) into `∑_w N^w_{s,t*} χ_w(g)` with\n275 | `N^w ≥ 0`. After this decomposition, the integral over `g` can be evaluated using\n276 | character orthogonality, giving `∑_w N^w · |∫ f · χ_w|² ≥ 0`.\n277 | \n278 | This is why the dual map (added to the axiom on 2026-07-30) is essential: it converts\n279 | `conj(χ_t(g))` to `χ_{dual(t)}(g)`, allowing the CG decomposition to combine\n280 | `χ_s(g) · χ_{dual(t)}(g)` into a single character sum.\n281 | \n282 | ## 5. Alternative Approach: Derived Axiom\n283 | \n284 | If the full formalization proves too complex, an intermediate approach is:\n285 | \n286 | 1. **Axiomatize** the separable decomposition of the interface Boltzmann factor as a\n287 |    \"derived axiom\" (a consequence of Peter-Weyl).\n288 | 2. **Prove** `transferMatrixPositivity_axiom` from the derived axiom (pure measure\n289 |    theory + character orthogonality). This removes `transferMatrixPositivity_axiom`,\n290 |    replacing it with the derived axiom (count stays at 6).\n291 | 3. **Prove** the derived axiom from `peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette` (pure\n292 |    combinatorics/representation theory). This removes the derived axiom (count → 5).\n293 | \n294 | This decomposes the problem into two cleaner pieces: the measure-theory part (step 2)\n295 | and the combinatorics part (step 3). The measure-theory part is conceptually cleaner\n296 | and may be more tractable.\n297 | \n298 | ## 6. Current State of Proved Ingredients\n299 | \n300 | All of the following are proved with 0 sorries and 0 custom axioms (verified by\n301 | `#print axioms`: only `propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`):\n302 | \n303 | - `plaquetteBoltzmannPD` — plaquette Boltzmann factor is PD on SU(N)⁴\n304 | - `plaquetteBoltzmannPD_inv` — with inverted 3rd/4th links\n305 | - `boltzmannFactorPD` — full Boltzmann factor is PD\n306 | - `osG_thetaG_factorization` — clean algebraic factorization of G(U)·G(θU)\n307 | - `reflectLinkVariable_measurePreserving` — reflection is measure-preserving\n308 | - `haarMeasure_inv_invariant` — Haar measure invariant under inversion\n309 | - `character_expansion_positivity` — abstract measure-theory scaffold\n310 | - `character_expansion_nonneg` — corollary: integral is non-negative\n311 | - `charProduct_PD` — product of two chars is PD via CG\n312 | - `charProduct_finset_decomp` — finite product of chars decomposes via CG\n313 | - `charSum_product_decomp` — product of char sums decomposes via CG\n314 | - `charSum_finprod_decomp` — finite product of char sums decomposes via CG\n315 | - `charSum_product_link_decomp` — product of per-link char sums → separable\n316 | - `charProduct_finset_decomp'` — generalized CG decomposition (appearances)\n317 | - `charProduct_link_separable_decomp` — per-term separable decomposition\n318 | - `charProduct_mixed_finset_decomp'` — mixed-conjugation CG decomposition\n319 | - `charProduct_mixed_link_separable_decomp` — per-term separable with mixed conj\n320 | - `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg` — key identity (measure theory, 0 sorries)\n321 | \n322 | ## 7. Axiom Inventory (6 axioms)\n323 | \n324 | 1. `peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette` — Peter-Weyl + CG + dual map (strengthened)\n325 | 2. `transferMatrixPositivity_axiom` — **TARGET: prove from #1 + #6**\n326 | 3. `os_reconstruction_theorem` — OS reconstruction\n327 | 4. `continuum_limit_exists` — continuum limit\n328 | 5. `mass_gap_axiom` — **THE CONJECTURE (do NOT remove)**\n329 | 6. `characterOrthogonality` — Schur orthogonality\n330 | \n331 | Closing `transferMatrixPositivity_axiom` reduces the count to **5**.\n\n---\n","timestamp":1785482865,"token_count":17180,"tool_call_id":"68fe930f-a642-4ea2-aabf-7ee8c0aa3ffc","full_token_estimate":11277,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"read_files","file_path":"docs/gap_analysis.md, docs/transfer_matrix_positivity_design.md","old_text":null,"new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":931,"byte_count":56323,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":40,"edit_line":null},"turn":3,"is_prune_marker":false}
