∑_w F(w) · [∏_{l ∈ u-links} χ_{w(l)}(g_l)] · [∏_{l ∈ V⁺-links} χ_{w'(l)}(g_l)]\n```\n\nwhere `w'` is `w` with dual applied to the time-like V⁺ links.\n\nNow, the integral becomes:\n```\n⟨g, Tg⟩ = ∑_w F(w) · [∫_u g(u) · ∏_{l ∈ u-links} χ_{w(l)}(g_l) dμ⁺⁰(u)] · [∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · ∏_{l ∈ V⁺-links} χ_{w'(l)}(g_l) dμ⁺(V⁺)]\n```\n\nWait, but this separation requires that the u-links and V⁺-links are independent, which they are (disjoint sets, product measures). But the issue is that `g(V⁺, σ(u⁰))` depends on BOTH V⁺ and u⁰ (the interface part of u). So the integral doesn't factor cleanly.\n\nHmm, this is the obstruction 1 from the gap_analysis: `θ⁻⁰` depends on both `x` and `y`. After the change of variables, this manifests as `g(V⁺, σ(u⁰))` depending on both V⁺ and u⁰.\n\nOK so this is genuinely hard. Let me think about how the Osterwalder-Seiler proof actually handles this.\n\nActually, I think the key insight is that the σ reflection on interface links is what makes this work. Let me think about it differently.\n\nThe transfer matrix T acts on functions of `u = (U⁺, u⁰)`. The kernel is:\n```\n(Tψ)(u) = ∫ K(u, v) · ψ(v) dμ(v)\n```\n\nFor T to be positive, we need `K(u, v) = ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(v))` with `a_w ≥ 0`.\n\nThe key is that the kernel K(u, v) must be **Hermitian** in the sense that `K(u, v) = conj(K(v, u))`. This is what makes the operator self-adjoint and the quadratic form `⟨g, Tg⟩` real.\n\nNow, the reflection symmetry `S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)) = S_int(V⁺, σ(u⁰), reflect(U⁺))` (from the design doc §2.4) is what gives this Hermitian property.\n\nLet me think about the actual structure. After the change of variables, the transfer matrix is:\n```\n(Tψ)(u) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫_{V⁺} ψ(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2 + S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)))) dμ⁺(V⁺)\n```\n\nHmm, but this isn't quite in the standard kernel form because of the σ(u⁰) in ψ. Let me think about what the \"kernel\" actually is.\n\nActually, I think the issue is that the transfer matrix T doesn't act on functions of `u = (U⁺, u⁰)` in the standard way. The σ reflection on u⁰ means that T is not a simple integral operator with kernel K(u, v).\n\nLet me reconsider. Maybe the right way to think about this is:\n\nThe transfer matrix T acts on the Hilbert space L²(μ⁺ × μ⁰) of functions of (U⁺, u⁰). The kernel is:\n```\n(Tψ)(U⁺, u⁰) = exp(-β·S⁺(U⁺, u⁰)/2) · ∫_{V⁺} ψ(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2 + S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)))) dμ⁺(V⁺)\n```\n\nThe σ(u⁰) in ψ means that T is not a standard integral operator — it involves a \"twist\" by σ on the interface. This is the obstruction.\n\nBut the Osterwalder-Seiler proof handles this by showing that T = B*·B where B is defined via the character expansion. The key is that the σ twist is absorbed into the character expansion.\n\nOK, I think I need to look at the actual Osterwalder-Seiler proof to understand the structure. Let me search the literature.\n\nActually, let me take a step back. This is a very deep mathematical problem. The formalization of steps (c) and (d) is the \"fundamental mathematical gap\" that the README and gap_analysis describe. \n\nLet me think about what's realistically achievable in this session. The design doc §5 suggests an \"Alternative Approach: Derived Axiom\" which decomposes the problem into:\n1. Axiomatize the separable decomposition (step a — already done as a lemma, not an axiom).\n2. Prove `transferMatrixPositivity_axiom` from the derived axiom (measure theory + character orthogonality).\n3. Prove the derived axiom from Peter-Weyl (combinatorics — already done as `plaquette_product_separable_decomp`).\n\nThe key question is: can we formalize step 2 — the measure-theory part — in a clean way?\n\nLet me think about what the abstract lemma for step 2 would look like. We need:\n\n**Abstract Lemma:** Given:\n- A transfer matrix T with kernel K(u, v) that has a separable decomposition `K(u, v) = ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(v))` with `a_w ≥ 0`.\n- The inner product `⟨g, Tg⟩ = ∫ g(u) · (Tg)(u) dμ(u)`.\n\nThen `⟨g, Tg⟩ = ∑_w a_w · |∫ g · Φ_w dμ|² ≥ 0`.\n\nThis is essentially `character_expansion_positivity` but in the operator-theoretic setting. The key difference from `character_expansion_positivity` is that here we don't have the θ reflection issue — the separable decomposition already handles it.\n\nBut wait, the transfer matrix kernel after the change of variables is NOT in the standard form `K(u, v) = ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(v))`. It has the σ twist. So we need to either:\n(a) Show that the σ twist can be absorbed into the separable decomposition, or\n(b) Work with a modified abstract lemma that handles the σ twist.\n\nHmm, let me think about this differently. Let me look at the actual form of the transfer matrix after the change of variables more carefully.\n\nActually, I think the right approach is to define the transfer matrix as an operator and show it equals B*·B. Let me think about what B would be.\n\nThe character expansion of the interface Boltzmann factor gives:\n```\nexp(-β·S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺))) = ∑_w F(w) · ∏_l χ_{w(l)}(g_l)\n```\n\nwhere the product is over all links in the interface plaquettes. The links include:\n- U⁺ links (positive)\n- u⁰ links (interface)\n- reflect(V⁺) links (reflected negative = positive with inversions for time-like)\n\nNow, the key is that the reflect(V⁺) links, after the inversions are handled by the dual map, give characters of V⁺ links (without inversion). So the separable decomposition separates into:\n```\nexp(-β·S_int) = ∑_w F(w) · [∏_{l ∈ U⁺∪u⁰} χ_{w(l)}(g_l)] · [∏_{l ∈ V⁺} χ_{w'(l)}(g_l)]\n```\n\nwhere w' is w with dual applied to time-like V⁺ links.\n\nNow, the transfer matrix is:\n```\n(Tψ)(u) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫_{V⁺} ψ(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2 + S_int)) dμ⁺(V⁺)\n```\n\nSubstituting the separable decomposition:\n```\n(Tψ)(u) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∑_w F(w) · [∏_{l ∈ U⁺∪u⁰} χ_{w(l)}(g_l)] · ∫_{V⁺} ψ(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2) · [∏_{l ∈ V⁺} χ_{w'(l)}(g_l)] dμ⁺(V⁺)\n```\n\nNow, the inner product:\n```\n⟨g, Tg⟩ = ∫_u g(u) · (Tg)(u) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n         = ∑_w F(w) · ∫_u g(u) · exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · [∏_{l ∈ U⁺∪u⁰} χ_{w(l)}(g_l)] · \n           [∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2) · ∏_{l ∈ V⁺} χ_{w'(l)}(g_l) dμ⁺(V⁺)] dμ⁺⁰(u)\n```\n\nThe issue is that the V⁺ integral depends on u⁰ (through σ(u⁰) in g and S⁺). So we can't separate the u and V⁺ integrals.\n\nBUT — here's the key insight from the OS proof: the σ reflection on u⁰ is exactly what makes this work. The function `g(V⁺, σ(u⁰))` involves σ(u⁰), and the character `∏_{l ∈ u⁰} χ_{w(l)}(g_l)` involves u⁰. The character orthogonality then relates these.\n\nActually, I think the right way to see this is:\n\nThe integral over u⁰ of `g(u⁺, u⁰) · χ_{w}(u⁰) · [∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · ... dμ⁺]` can be evaluated by changing variables `u⁰ ↦ σ(u⁰)` in the inner integral (using the measure-preserving property of σ on interface links). This gives:\n\n```\n∫_{u⁰} g(u⁺, u⁰) · χ_{w}(u⁰) · [∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, u⁰) · ... dμ⁺] dμ⁰(u⁰)\n```\n\nWait, that's not quite right either. Let me think more carefully.\n\nActually, I think the σ reflection is measure-preserving on the interface (it inverts time-like links, and Haar measure is inversion-invariant by `haarMeasure_inv_invariant`). So we can change variables `u⁰ ↦ σ(u⁰)` in the u⁰ integral.\n\nLet me denote:\n- `A_w(u⁺, u⁰) = g(u⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺, u⁰)/2) · ∏_{l ∈ U⁺} χ_{w(l)}(g_l)`\n- `B_w(V⁺, u⁰) = g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2) · ∏_{l ∈ V⁺} χ_{w'(l)}(g_l)`\n\nThen:\n```\n⟨g, Tg⟩ = ∑_w F(w) · ∫_{u⁺} ∫_{u⁰} A_w(u⁺, u⁰) · χ_{w}(u⁰) · [∫_{V⁺} B_w(V⁺, u⁰) dμ⁺(V⁺)] dμ⁰(u⁰) dμ⁺(u⁺)\n```\n\nwhere `χ_{w}(u⁰) = ∏_{l ∈ u⁰} χ_{w(l)}(g_l)`.\n\nNow, change variables `u⁰ ↦ σ(u⁰)` in the u⁰ integral (using σ measure-preserving):\n```\n= ∑_w F(w) · ∫_{u⁺} ∫_{u⁰} A_w(u⁺, σ(u⁰)) · χ_{w}(σ(u⁰)) · [∫_{V⁺} B_w(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) dμ⁺(V⁺)] dμ⁰(u⁰) dμ⁺(u⁺)\n```\n\nNow, `χ_{w}(σ(u⁰)) = ∏_{l ∈ u⁰} χ_{w(l)}(σ(g_l))`. For time-like interface links, `σ(g_l) = g_l⁻¹`, so `χ_{w(l)}(g_l⁻¹) = conj(χ_{w(l)}(g_l)) = χ_{dual(w(l))}(g_l)`. For spatial interface links, `σ(g_l) = g_l`, so `χ_{w(l)}(g_l)`.\n\nSo `χ_{w}(σ(u⁰)) = conj(χ_{w}(u⁰))` (up to the dual map).\n\nAnd `B_w(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) = g(V⁺, σ(σ(u⁰))) · ... = g(V⁺, u⁰) · ...` (since σ is an involution).\n\nSo:\n```\n= ∑_w F(w) · ∫_{u⁺} ∫_{u⁰} A_w(u⁺, σ(u⁰)) · conj(χ_{w}(u⁰)) · [∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺, u⁰)/2) · ∏_{l ∈ V⁺} χ_{w'(l)}(g_l) dμ⁺(V⁺)] dμ⁰(u⁰) dμ⁺(u⁺)\n```\n\nHmm, this is getting very complicated. And `A_w(u⁺, σ(u⁰))` still involves σ(u⁰), which means the u⁺ and u⁰ integrals don't separate cleanly.\n\nI think I'm going down a rabbit hole. Let me step back and think about what's actually tractable.\n\nLet me re-read the design doc's step (c) and (d) descriptions:\n\n**Step (c):** Use CG with dual representations + character orthogonality to evaluate the integrals and obtain `∑_w a_w · |Fourier coefficient|² ≥ 0`.\n\n**Step (d):** Conclude T = B*·B and ⟨g, Tg⟩ = ‖Bg‖² ≥ 0.\n\nThese are described as the remaining work. The design doc §5 suggests the \"Derived Axiom\" approach as an alternative if the full formalization is too complex.\n\nI think the most tractable next step is to follow the \"Derived Axiom\" approach:\n\n1. **State a derived axiom** that captures the key mathematical fact: the transfer matrix kernel has a separable decomposition with non-negative coefficients, in a form that handles the σ reflection.\n\n2. **Prove `transferMatrixPositivity_axiom` from the derived axiom** using measure theory + character orthogonality. This is the clean measure-theory part.\n\n3. **Prove the derived axiom from `peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette`** using `plaquette_product_separable_decomp` + lattice combinatorics. This is the combinatorics part (partially done by step (a)).\n\nBut actually, I realize that even step 2 is non-trivial because of the σ reflection. Let me think about what the derived axiom should look like.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Maybe the cleanest approach is to define the operator B explicitly and show T = B*·B. Let me think about what B would be.\n\nIn the OS proof, B is the \"Fourier coefficient extraction\" operator. Given a function g on the interface config space, B extracts its Fourier coefficients with respect to the character basis. Then T = B*·B means ⟨g, Tg⟩ = ‖Bg‖².\n\nBut defining B requires the character expansion of the interface Boltzmann factor, which is the lattice-specific part.\n\nOK, I think I need to be more concrete. Let me look at the actual code to understand the structure of the transfer matrix, the interface action, and the relevant definitions. Then I can figure out the most tractable next step.\n\nLet me read the key definitions in TransferMatrix.lean: `transferMatrixCorrect`, `g_posInterface`, `osG`, `PosInterfaceConfig`, etc.\n\nLet me also look at the `characterOrthogonality` axiom and `character_expansion_positivity` to understand what's available.\n\nLet me read the beginning of TransferMatrix.lean to understand the definitions.","turn":20,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":33,"role":"Tool","content":"C:\\github\\YangMills\\src\\lean\\YangMills\\Proofs\\TransferMatrix.lean\n-- 2973 lines, 109419 bytes --\n  1 | /-\n  2 | \n  3 | # Transfer Matrix: Corrected Approach\n  4 | \n  5 | \n  6 | \n  7 | This file provides definitions and lemmas toward a transfer matrix proof\n  8 | \n  9 | of the Osterwalder-Seiler reflection positivity for the periodic lattice.\n 10 | \n 11 | \n 12 | \n 13 | ## Mathematical Setup\n 14 | \n 15 | \n 16 | \n 17 | Let μ₀ = μ⁺ ⊗ μ⁻ ⊗ μ⁰ be the factorization of the product Haar measure on\n 18 | \n 19 | the finite periodic lattice into positive-time, negative-time, and interface\n 20 | \n 21 | links (via `measure_factorization'`).\n 22 | \n 23 | \n 24 | \n 25 | For an observable f depending only on links in the positive-time and interface\n 26 | \n 27 | regions (`dependsOnlyOnPosInterface N T L f`), define:\n 28 | \n 29 | \n 30 | \n 31 |     G(U) = f(U)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(U))·exp(-β·S_OS_int(U)/2)\n 32 | \n 33 |     g(u) = f(u)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(u)/2)   for u ∈ PosInterfaceConfig\n 34 | \n 35 | \n 36 | \n 37 | The reflection positivity lemma `gibbsExpectationPeriodic_reflection_positive`\n 38 | \n 39 | reduces to proving:\n 40 | \n 41 | \n 42 | \n 43 |     ∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀(U) ≥ 0\n 44 | \n 45 | \n 46 | \n 47 | ## The Correct Transfer Matrix\n 48 | \n 49 | \n 50 | \n 51 | Define the transfer matrix T acting on functions ψ : PosInterfaceConfig → ℝ by:\n 52 | \n 53 | \n 54 | \n 55 |     (T ψ)(U⁺,U⁰) = ∫ ψ(θ⁻⁰(U⁻,U⁰))\n 56 | \n 57 |                      · exp(-β·(S_OS⁻(U⁻)+S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰))/2)\n 58 | \n 59 |                      dμ⁻(U⁻)\n 60 | \n 61 | \n 62 | \n 63 | where θ⁻⁰(U⁻,U⁰) is the restriction of θ(merge(U⁻,U⁰)) to positive+interface\n 64 | \n 65 | sites (i.e., reflecting the negative links to the positive side).\n 66 | \n 67 | \n 68 | \n 69 | ## Key Identity\n 70 | \n 71 | \n 72 | \n 73 |     ∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀(U) = ∫_{PosInt} g(u)·(T g)(u) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n 74 | \n 75 | \n 76 | \n 77 | This holds because:\n 78 | \n 79 | 1. G(U)·G(θU) = g(U⁺,U⁰)·g(θ⁻⁰(U⁻,U⁰))\n 80 | \n 81 |                 · exp(-β·(S_OS⁻(U⁻)+S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰))/2)\n 82 | \n 83 | 2. The factorization μ₀ ≅ μ⁺ × μ⁻ × μ⁰ turns the LHS into an integral\n 84 | \n 85 |    over U⁺,U⁻,U⁰ of this expression.\n 86 | \n 87 | 3. Integrating out U⁻ gives (T g)(U⁺,U⁰) by definition.\n 88 | \n 89 | \n 90 | \n 91 | ## Positivity\n 92 | \n 93 | \n 94 | \n 95 | The operator T is POSITIVE because its integral kernel is a positive-definite\n 96 | \n 97 | function on (SU(N)ⁿ × SU(N)ⁿ). This follows from the Peter-Weyl theorem on\n 98 | \n 99 | SU(N) (Osterwalder-Seiler 1979, Theorem 3.1). Therefore ⟨g, T·g⟩ ≥ 0.\n100 | \n\n... 2873 more line(s) below (use offset=101 to continue reading)","timestamp":1785484614,"token_count":810,"tool_call_id":"3ad319f6-766c-43c5-9310-6f41bda7d571","full_token_estimate":693,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"read_file","file_path":"src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/TransferMatrix.lean","old_text":null,"new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":2973,"byte_count":109419,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":0,"edit_line":null},"turn":20,"is_prune_marker":false}
